WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.180
MINDELL: Good afternoon.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.870
It's my pleasure to
introduce and to welcome you

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:26.600
to the MIT 150 symposium on
brains, minds, and machines.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.130
My name is David
Mindell, and I'm

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.950
chair of the MIT 150
Steering Committee,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:36.350
and I just wanted to say a
few words about MIT's 150th

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:40.610
that provide a little bit of
context for today's symposium.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.630
In 1853, William Barton Rogers
came north from Virginia

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.250
to pursue his dream of a new
kind of technical education,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.930
an education that would
mix the world of science

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.810
and the useful arts,
theory and practice, what

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:58.250
we have come to know as mens
et manus, or mind and hand.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.641
Nine years later-- eight
years later, in 1861,

00:01:02.641 --> 00:01:04.849
the governor of Massachusetts
fulfilled Roger's dream

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:09.380
by signing MIT'S charter
on April 10 of that year,

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.300
creating this unique
and innovative

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.790
educational institution.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:17.040
Now, 150 years and
two weeks later,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.860
we're celebrating
MIT's accomplishments

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.280
in a whole variety of fields
of ideas and inventions

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.920
that changed our world and
helped define it today.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.660
And of the courageous
professors,

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.930
students, graduates, and alumni
who have gone forth from this

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:33.730
place to make their
contributions to the world.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.270
The MIT 150 celebrations
include 150 days

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.600
of events, concerts,
other festivals.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.070
Today, we're on the 117th day,
and we've already begun to see,

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.750
over the past 117 days, the
emergence of tomorrow's MIT,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.900
one that's united by
ambitious intellectual agendas

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.870
and focused on both its
core research domains

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.340
and on the Institute's
relationship

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.170
to the wider world.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:06.420
Within the 150th anniversary,
the intellectual [? core ?]

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.100
is a series that
comprises the core of MIT.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.620
Thinkers students,
researchers, and professors

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.740
talking about great ideas,
contemplating the world,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.870
and perhaps even making
a little progress on some

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.910
of the interesting problems.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.020
This is the essence of 150
symposia series, the sixth

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.750
and last of which
we're opening today.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.940
The other symposia have
focused on economics

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.017
and finance back in
January, conquering cancer

00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:35.100
through the convergence
of science and engineering

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.610
in March, women's leadership
in science and engineering,

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.028
also at the end of
March, computation

00:02:41.028 --> 00:02:43.070
and the transformation of
practically everything,

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:46.160
two weeks ago, the
future of exploration

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.180
in earth, air, ocean,
and space last week,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.770
and today's brains,
minds, and machines.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.840
Each of these symposia were
chosen for the leading faculty,

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.690
the exciting ideas, the new
ideas, and the symposium's

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.880
focus on more than one
department and more

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.440
than one school.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.870
Of course they in no
way cover the full range

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.510
of the interesting research
that goes on at the Institute,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.250
but they all represent
cutting-edge work

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:15.210
that epitomizes what
is best about MIT.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.250
My thanks particularly
today to professors

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.207
Irene Heim, Tommy Poggio,
and Josh Tenenbaum

00:03:20.207 --> 00:03:22.290
for their willingness to
step forward and organize

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.090
this historic gathering.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.220
And to all of our
participants and all of you

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.160
for taking the time to be here
to share your insights as well.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.410
On a personal note, I find
this symposium particularly

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.605
exciting, not least
because I've spent

00:03:35.605 --> 00:03:36.980
a good deal of my
career studying

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.320
the prehistory of
cybernetics and currently

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.990
work on topics of
human and machine

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:45.630
relationships in
complex systems,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.930
but also because from the
very first pre-proposal

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.610
that we reviewed
on this symposium,

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.980
we saw how it coincides
with the new intelligence

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.970
initiative at MIT and represents
a genuinely new synthesis.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.410
I could not think
of a better way

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.690
to culminate the
MIT 150 symposium

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.870
series than with this event.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.480
Nothing could better illustrate
MIT's seminal history

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.780
in these areas, its synthetic
intellectual culture,

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.010
and the great promise
for the future

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.630
than what you're about to see
in the following two days.

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:20.420
I'll now introduce the Dean of
Science at MIT, Marc Kastner.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:20.920
Thank you.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.640
KASTNER: Thank you, David.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.750
David said it well.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:36.910
This is an extremely
exciting topic.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:41.890
I love it in particular
because in addition

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.505
to the deep intellectual
challenges, it already--

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.250
the new intelligence
initiative has already

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:55.630
involved faculty members from
all of the five schools at MIT.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.960
The School of Science, with
its brain and cognitive science

00:04:58.960 --> 00:04:59.965
department.

00:04:59.965 --> 00:05:02.690
The School of
Engineering, obviously.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.000
But also the School of
Humanities, Arts and Social

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.930
Sciences, where linguistics, and
philosophy, and economics sit.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.870
The Sloan School of
Business, and the School

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:21.430
of Architecture Urban Planning,
which houses the Media Lab.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.640
So this is an
intellectual challenge

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.010
which draws together faculty
members from all across MIT,

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.240
and that makes it very exciting.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.330
So this problem has a
great history at MIT,

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:38.320
but also has a
great future at MIT.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.040
I want to add my thanks to
professors Heim, Poggio,

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:43.270
and Tenenbaum.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:47.710
And a special thanks to Lore and
Pat McGovern and the McGovern

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:50.920
Institute for hosting
a reception tomorrow.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.540
So on with the show.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:53.260
It should be fun.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.870
POGGIO: So on behalf of my
co-organizers, Irene Heim

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:14.360
and Josh Tenenbaum, and all the
MIT 150 organization and people

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.750
of my group that will
be helping you around

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.850
in the next couple
of days, I would

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.630
like to welcome you
to this symposium

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:27.270
and to this first
panel of today.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:31.700
We are in a typical
gritty MIT classroom.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:37.010
We kept you outside in the heat
just to emphasize the point.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:42.560
We are very close to where the
old barracks were, Building 20.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.710
Back in the '50s
and '60s there was

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.080
a remarkable
intellectual activity

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.280
in the old country that
started around the new physics

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.490
and engineering of electronics
and information processing.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:00.470
And if MIT was one of the
main centers of this whirlwind

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:05.330
of activity, Building 20
was really the center of it,

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.980
serving as a magical incubator
for a number of fields

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:14.540
as diverse as information
theory, cybernetics,

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:19.360
neural networks, linguistics,
artificial intelligence,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.720
and neuroscience.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.720
Researchers in the
building included

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:29.330
Claude Shannon, Norbert Wiener,
Walter Pitts, McCulloch,

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:33.050
Noam Chomsky, Maurice
[? Harley, ?] Jerry Lettvin,

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:34.580
Marvin Minsky.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.740
And the intellectual
[? ferment ?] eventually

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.810
converged in the
ambitious attempt

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:41.930
to understanding intelligence
and replicating it

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.330
in computers and machines.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.790
Marvin Minsky and
Seymour Papert to

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.890
were key in starting
the new field

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.170
of artificial intelligence.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:55.070
Noam Chomsky and
David Marr pioneered

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.140
linguistics, and
cognitive science,

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.450
and [? computation ?]
neuroscience.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:03.650
And I would like to remember in
particular Jerry Lettvin, who

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:06.560
died about a week ago.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.800
He was part of that group.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:16.380
He was one of the giants in
this MIT history of research

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.850
between the brain and the mind.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.720
He had a unique culture.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.600
He was unique.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.210
He was loved by everybody.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.870
We all miss him a lot.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:34.039
Artificial intelligence
started 50 years ago, about.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.760
Machine learning started
growing under the name

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.220
of neural networks
about 25 years ago.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.240
And academic research in these
two areas is one of the main

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:49.530
reasons for the emergence in the
last five years, I would say,

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.740
of remarkably
successful applications,

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:57.070
commercial and not commercial,
like Deep Blue, Google search,

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.950
[? Kinect, ?] Watson
[? Mobileye. ?]

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:02.160
Each of these systems--

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.660
computers that play
chess, search the web,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:09.590
recognize human gestures,
answer to [? Jeopardy, ?] allow

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:15.810
cars to see, is at human-level
performance in a narrow domain.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:18.510
But none of these systems can
be said to be intelligent,

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.650
these intelligence.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.280
And the problem of intelligence,
and of how the brain produces

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.610
intelligence, and how to make
really intelligent machines,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.160
is still wide open.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.050
So the main thesis
to be discussed

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:38.100
in this symposium, the way
Josh and I thought about it,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:43.140
is that a new effort in
curiosity-driven research,

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:45.570
basic research,
is needed in order

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.550
to understand intelligence
and understand the brain.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:53.690
That this new basic research
should, this time, not only

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:56.120
rely on computer
science, but also

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:59.870
integrated with neuroscience
and cognitive science.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.860
And it should also integrate
tightly different aspects,

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:09.090
facets, of intelligence, such
as vision, planning, language,

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:10.860
social intelligence.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:14.510
So I believe that
50 years later, it

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.150
is the time to try again.

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.700
This symposium is
a way to find out

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.550
whether we all agree that it
is indeed time to try again,

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.756
and if so how?

00:10:25.756 --> 00:10:30.840
So now, I will start
the real symposium.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.230
And I have the
pleasure to introduce

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:38.880
the moderator of this
extraordinary panel, Steve

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:40.230
Pinker.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.770
You all know Steve, so he
does not need an introduction.

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:48.960
You all know him from his books,
starting with The Language

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.840
Instinct, a great book.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.520
Steve was a colleague in
my department here at MIT,

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.920
and now we are loaning him back
for a few days from Harvard.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:00.420
Steve.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:12.700
PINKER: Thank you, Tony.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:14.160
Welcome to the golden age.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.470
I'll look at the original roots
of artificial intelligence,

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:20.370
cognitive science,
and neuroscience.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.860
Moderating this panel
is a daunting challenge

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:28.000
that puts me in mind of the
famous quotation from John F.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.640
Kennedy when he hosted a
dinner at the White House

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.460
for all of the living
Nobel Laureates

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.295
of the Western hemisphere.

00:11:35.295 --> 00:11:37.600
And he said "this is the
most extraordinary collection

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.640
of talent and human
knowledge that has ever

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.560
been gathered together
at the White House,

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:44.500
with the possible exception
of when Thomas Jefferson dined

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.440
alone."

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:51.672
It's not clear who the Thomas
Jefferson figure equivalent

00:11:51.672 --> 00:11:53.380
would be in this case,
although I imagine

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.297
there might be some
people in the audience who

00:11:55.297 --> 00:11:57.460
would nominate our
late colleague,

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:00.763
the inimitable Jerry Lettvin.

00:12:00.763 --> 00:12:02.680
The second reason this
is a daunting challenge

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:05.850
is that our distinguished
panelists represent

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.980
a remarkable diversity
of interests,

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:12.910
from the biology of soil
roundworms to the nature

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:15.460
of logic, truth, and meaning.

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:19.030
Nonetheless, I think there is a
common thread that all of them

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:22.270
are contributors to
what I think of as one

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:25.060
of the great revolutions
in human thought, which

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:28.810
is the scientific and
understanding of life and mind

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:31.570
in terms of information,
computation,

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.910
and feedback and control.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.460
Well into the 20th
century, life and mind

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.830
remained scientific mysteries.

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:42.460
Life was thought of as this
mysterious substance called

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:46.300
protoplasm, some kind
of quivering gel,

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.910
animated by an élan vital.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.900
The mind was thought of
as a portion of some realm

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.280
of the soul or spirit.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.220
Or, according to the
dogma of behaviorism,

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:00.100
something that didn't exist
at all, just one big category

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:00.970
error.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.390
But then, in the middle
decades of the 20th century,

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:08.740
ideas of thinkers like Turing,
Church, von Neumann, Wiener,

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.590
Shannon, Weaver,
McCulloch, and Pitts

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.870
gave us a rigorous
language in which

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:17.920
to understand the concepts of
information and computation

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.910
and apply them to
domesticate these formerly

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:26.830
mysterious realms, in the
process revolutionizing biology

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:28.120
and psychology.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:31.870
They gave us what became the
insight that the stuff of life

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:35.200
is not some magical
protoplasm, but rather

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.870
matter that's organized
by information.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:40.990
And today, when we
discuss heredity,

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:43.600
we use the language
of linguistics.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.370
We talk about the genetic code.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:48.280
We talk about DNA
sequences being

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:51.580
synonymous, or meaningless,
or palindromic,

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.740
or stored in libraries.

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:58.210
Even the relation between
hereditary information

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:01.780
and the actual meat and
juices of the organism we

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:04.960
explain with concepts
from information, namely

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:07.510
transcription and translation.

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:10.570
The metaphor is profound.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:13.120
Similarly, the stuff
of thought is no longer

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.430
thought to be some
kind of ghostly spirit,

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:18.940
nor a mirage or category
error, but also can

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:21.190
be understood in
terms of information.

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:24.250
That beliefs are a
kind of representation,

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:27.820
thinking a kind of
computation or transformation,

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:31.990
and action a problem of control,
in the engineer's sense.

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:34.630
These ideas we take
for granted now,

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:37.420
but I am always
struck going back

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:40.990
to earlier great thinkers
in biology and psychology,

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.270
how much they
floundered without it.

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.880
When one reads great
philosophers of mind,

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:49.390
like Hume, or great
biologists like Darwin,

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:52.870
I often wish that I could
reach back over the centuries

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.240
and tell them a few things
about the modern science

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.780
of information, because one
could see that they were

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:03.250
flailing around with hydraulic
and mechanical analogies

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:08.530
that could be so clearly
explicated by what we know now

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.900
about information
and computation.

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:14.890
I think it was the 1950s and
early 1960s that was a turning

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:19.120
point in both fields, and the
six people in today's panel

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.490
were all, in different
ways, instrumental

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:23.060
in making it happen.

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:25.360
I don't think I'm going
to offend anyone's vanity

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:29.170
by introducing them and inviting
them to speak in order of age.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:34.780
Sydney Brenner is a biologist.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:38.340
All of us know that 58 years--

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.390
BRENNER: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:43.900
PINKER: Everyone knows that
58 years ago Watson and Crick

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.300
explicated the structure of DNA.

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:49.900
But the DNA would
be useless if there

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:51.760
wasn't some way
for the information

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:54.070
that it contained
to actually affect

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:56.830
the development and
functioning of the organism.

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:58.900
The discovery of
the genetic code

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.530
is something that we owe to our
first speaker, Sydney Brenner,

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:07.240
working in collaboration with
Francis Crick and others.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:11.630
As if that weren't
enough, the discovery

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:13.970
of the operation
of the genetic code

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:18.260
and mechanisms of
transcription into RNA

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:21.350
and translation into
protein, Sydney Brenner

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:24.980
was also instrumental in the
modern science of development.

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.560
How the information
coded in the genes

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:30.230
actually builds a
three-dimensional functioning

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:33.200
origin, and, for that
matter, neuroscience,

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:38.360
both through his choice
of the lowly roundworm,

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.220
Caenorhabditis elegans.

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:43.880
C. elegans, often known
as Sydney's worm, which

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:48.050
has exactly 959
cells, 302 neurons,

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:52.010
and therefore offers a perfect
opportunity to reverse engineer

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.310
the process of
development and the wiring

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:57.260
of the nervous system.

00:16:57.260 --> 00:17:00.110
In recognition of
this accomplishment,

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:03.410
Sydney was awarded
the 2002 Nobel Prize

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.540
in Physiology or Medicine.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:08.180
But perhaps an even
greater recognition

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:12.170
is that he has been immortalized
in the Linnaean taxonomy,

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:16.579
a sister species of C. elegans,
Caenorhabditis brenneri,

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:18.940
has been named after
Sydney Brenner.

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:21.170
Sydney is senior
distinguished fellow

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:23.630
at the Crick-Jacobs center
at the Salk Institute

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:25.940
for Biological Studies.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.640
Marvin Minsky is a
computer scientist.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:31.070
And he is widely
recognized as one

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.660
of the founders,
perhaps the founder,

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:35.420
of artificial intelligence,
cognitive science,

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:36.830
and robotics.

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:38.850
He is responsible,
among other things,

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.020
for the first neural network
simulator, the first computer

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:44.840
simulation of semantic
memory, one of the first music

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:47.390
synthesizers, first
mechanical arm,

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:51.200
and the first
programmable Logo Turtle.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:54.620
He has, since then, been
a major theoretician

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:58.040
on how to apply the
computational analysis

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:00.440
to problems such as vision,
reasoning, learning,

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:03.890
common sense, consciousness,
emotion, and so on.

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:05.810
He has been recognized
with the Association

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:08.180
of Computing Machinery's
Turing Prize,

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:10.220
as well as the Japan Prize.

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:13.880
But perhaps most
significantly, he

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:18.060
inspired Arthur C. Clarke when
he was writing 2001 a Space

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:22.220
Odyssey and served as
consultant for that movie,

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:25.310
in particular for
the computer HAL.

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:28.010
Marvin Minsky is Professor
of Media Arts and Sciences

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.500
Emeritus at MIT.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:33.770
Noam Chomsky is a linguist
who revolutionized

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:35.480
the study of
language by changing

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:39.170
the very nature of the questions
that the field attempts

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:40.340
to answer.

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:44.480
Noam pointed out that the most
profound mystery of language

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:48.080
is first of all that any
competent speaker can

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.960
produce or comprehend an
infinite number of novel

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:51.830
sentences.

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:54.200
Therefore, our
knowledge of language

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:58.550
has to be captured by a
recursive, generative system.

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:03.200
Noam explored the
mathematics of such systems,

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:05.270
has developed a
number of theories

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:07.520
of what kind of recursive
generative system

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:09.650
is implemented in
the human mind,

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:13.400
and inspired the modern
science of psycholinguistics

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:17.990
that explores how language
is processed in real-time.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:21.710
Noam also set, as a
problem for linguistics,

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:25.340
the project of figuring
out how children acquire

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:29.360
a first language without formal
instruction in an astonishingly

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:32.990
short period of time, suggesting
that children are innately

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:34.970
equipped with a
universal grammar

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:38.660
of the abstract principles
behind language,

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:40.940
an idea that led to the
modern science of language

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:41.890
acquisition.

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:45.320
Noam is also largely
responsible for the overthrow

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:50.750
of behaviorism as the main
dogma in the study of psychology

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:53.360
and philosophy,
for rehabilitating

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:56.300
the philosophical
approach of rationalism,

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:58.190
and for making the
concepts of innateness

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:01.610
and modularity respectable
in the study of mind.

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:05.210
Chomsky has been recognized
among many prizes,

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:07.910
with the Kyoto Prize, the
Helmholtz Medal, and the Ben

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:09.140
Franklin Medal.

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:13.460
He is also the most
cited living scholar,

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:15.800
according to citation
counts, and has

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:19.130
become such a fixture
of popular culture

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:23.930
that Woody Allen had him feature
prominently in his New Yorker

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:26.010
story "The Whore of Mensa".

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.478
And I believe that
Noam is the only MIT

00:20:28.478 --> 00:20:30.020
professor whose
lecture has ever been

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:31.970
featured on the
B-side of an album

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:33.580
by the rock group Chumbawumba.

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:38.780
Noam is Institute
Professor Emeritus

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:42.580
in the Department of Linguistics
and Philosophy here at MIT.

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:46.130
Emilio Bizzi is a
neuroscientist who

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:49.190
made early contributions to
our understanding of sleep,

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:51.830
but has spent most of
his career studying

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:55.010
the process of movement.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:58.190
Emilio is responsible
for setting

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:00.410
the agenda for the
neuroscience of movement,

00:21:00.410 --> 00:21:03.920
pointing out that the
movement of animals

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:06.440
is not simply a
set of knee jerks,

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:09.350
or pressing a button
and a lever moving,

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:13.580
but rather has goal-directedness
that a simple muscle

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:17.150
motion would be useless
if it was executed

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:20.270
the same way regardless of the
starting position of the body,

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.150
and rather that movements
have to be organized

00:21:23.150 --> 00:21:24.890
towards positions in space.

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:27.380
Movement is therefore
an intelligent process

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:31.580
at every level of
the nervous system.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:35.360
Emilio is also largely
responsible for the maturation

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.580
of neuroscience as a
field, which did not even

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:41.690
exist as a name when
he entered the field.

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:48.110
And was the founding director
of the Whitaker College at MIT,

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:49.730
and founding head
of the Department

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:51.770
of Brain and Cognitive
Sciences, in which

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:54.800
I was privileged to
teach for many years.

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:56.510
In addition to his
scientific brilliance,

00:21:56.510 --> 00:22:01.075
Emilio is widely respected as a
man of judgment and erudition,

00:22:01.075 --> 00:22:02.450
and his wisdom
has been tapped in

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:04.850
many scholarly organizations.

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:07.370
Together with prizes such
as the [? Empedocles ?]

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:10.850
Prize and the President of Italy
Gold Medal for Achievements

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.700
in Science Prize,
Emilio has served

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:17.090
as the president of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences.

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:20.210
Emilio is currently Institute
Professor of Neuroscience

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:21.950
here at MIT.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:23.990
Barbara Partee is a linguist.

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:27.140
She was a student
in the first class

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:32.420
of the fabled MIT graduate
program in linguistics.

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:35.960
And as the first recipient of
a National Science Foundation

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:39.800
Fellowship in linguistics, her
graduate career symbolically

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:43.160
inaugurates the
appearance of linguistics

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.920
as an official science.

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:49.490
Barbara transformed the field
of linguistics, which hitherto

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:52.400
had concentrated on
syntax and phonology,

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:55.100
by putting semantics,
the study of meaning,

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:57.620
on a formal,
rigorous foundation,

00:22:57.620 --> 00:23:00.320
tying linguistics to
the world of logic

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:03.060
and the concepts of
meaning and truth.

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:05.980
She remains the world's
foremost semanticist,

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:08.180
and moreover, trained
all of the world's

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:10.940
other foremost semanticists.

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:13.130
When she retired
recently, her students

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:15.800
presented her with
a genealogical tree

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:18.470
of all of the people
that she has trained.

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:20.720
They noted that it has
a depth of four, which

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:23.330
means that she has trained
several great, great,

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:27.740
grand students, and
it has 176 nodes.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:30.260
Barbara has been recognized
with the Max Planck Research

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:32.210
Award for the Max
Planck Society,

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:35.420
and is currently distinguished
University Professor Emerita,

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:39.200
that's feminine for Emeritus, at
the University of Massachusetts

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.240
at Amherst.

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:44.040
Patrick Winston is a
computer scientist.

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.500
He was one of the
first researchers

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:48.800
to give a rigorous analysis
of the concept of learning

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:51.350
in the framework of
modern symbolic artificial

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:54.860
intelligence, and his work
on the concept of learning

00:23:54.860 --> 00:23:57.380
continues to be
influential today.

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:59.570
He was also instrumental
in transforming

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.720
artificial intelligence
from a bunch of hackers

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:06.470
in plaid flannel shirts to
a respectable academic field

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:13.010
through his textbook, through
directing the Artificial

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:16.980
Intelligence Laboratory at
MIT for most of its existence,

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:19.250
and his famous course in
artificial intelligence,

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:21.590
as well as starting a
number of companies.

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:24.900
Patrick has been recognized by
a number of teaching awards,

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:28.310
including the MacVicar, the
Baker, and the Graduate Student

00:24:28.310 --> 00:24:31.160
Council Award here at MIT.

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:36.170
And is widely known and beloved,
aside from his contributions

00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:39.680
to artificial intelligence,
in his training of teachers

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:42.650
through his famous
IAP talk, "How

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:45.590
To Speak" which has
influenced many of us.

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:49.370
Among the many suggestions for
how to keep a class engaged,

00:24:49.370 --> 00:24:52.880
he suggested that every
lecturer should cultivate

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:57.290
some eccentricity, whether
it be wearing a rope belt,

00:24:57.290 --> 00:25:00.890
or tugging a lock of
your hair, or erasing

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:03.240
the Blackboard with both hands.

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.150
I was influenced by
this as a postdoc,

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:07.340
and ever since, I
have always lectured

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:08.495
wearing a gaudy necktie.

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:13.850
Patrick is Ford Professor
of Artificial Intelligence

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:17.550
and Computer Science at MIT.

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:21.650
I have asked the six panelists
to spend 10 minutes each,

00:25:21.650 --> 00:25:23.960
sharing any personal
thoughts that they think

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:27.530
the audience would enjoy on the
birth of the modern sciences

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:31.400
of life and mind, and their
reflections on the origins,

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:34.970
key questions, key discoveries,
and open challenges

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:35.940
of your field.

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:37.850
So we'll start with Sydney.

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:41.420
I'd like to ask each of you
to speak for about 10 minutes,

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:45.740
and then I will ask you
to amplify, reflect, ask

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:47.750
each other questions, and so on.

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:51.140
I'll put a little timer
here to just remind you

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:52.535
how much time has elapsed.

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:06.020
BRENNER: I've shrunk
in recent years.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:13.170
Well, I thought I'd give--

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:16.240
to explain why I'm
here, I thought

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:19.550
I'd give two classes of reasons.

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:23.320
The first will be
sentimental, and I'd

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:29.890
like to say that my association
with artificial intelligence

00:26:29.890 --> 00:26:34.690
has always been as an
interested spectator.

00:26:34.690 --> 00:26:38.680
I don't think I ever
played in the game.

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:43.600
My interest in it came
from a very long friendship

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:45.670
with Seymour Papert.

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:49.300
We grew up together
in South Africa.

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:54.730
We shared a room in the
Department of Physiology.

00:26:54.730 --> 00:26:58.390
I taught Seymour
neurophysiology.

00:26:58.390 --> 00:27:01.100
He taught me mathematics.

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:03.730
And I came to the conclusion
he was both the better

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:05.485
student and the better teacher.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:12.010
It was the connection
with Seymour,

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:14.920
that when I finally
grew up, that

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:20.980
brought me into association with
the Department of Artificial

00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:22.660
Intelligence.

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:29.240
And I sent one of my
best students here.

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:30.880
I wouldn't say he was a student.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:34.390
One of my best colleagues
here, David Marr.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:37.870
And so those are the reasons
why I've always been.

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:42.460
And in later years, which
I ask you to ponder on,

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:47.440
there was a great adventure
here producing something

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:50.000
called a thinking machine.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:53.860
In fact, there was a
corporation named after that.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:56.830
And I suppose that's
what we were after.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:00.580
We have to resuscitate
thinking machines.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:05.580
Now I'd like to give you the
mental reasons why I'm here.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:10.910
I am very influenced
in about 1950,

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:17.420
'51 it was, reading an
article by John von Neumann

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:21.530
called "The Theory of
Self-Reproducing Machines".

00:28:21.530 --> 00:28:26.420
It had been published in a
book called The Hixon Symposium

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:29.040
of Cerebral Mechanisms.

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:30.980
And that's a very
interesting book

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:35.600
to read through, because you
can see all the false leads.

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:40.490
So that paper from
Neumann went unattended to

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:46.340
at the actual basic logical
construct for the way

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:48.020
the genes work.

00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:52.880
And in fact, the whole of this
is done without any reference

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:54.350
to biology.

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:57.320
And indeed, when the
biologists discovered

00:28:57.320 --> 00:29:02.540
that this was the
machinery, then nobody

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:04.470
had mentioned von Neumann.

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:10.330
In fact, they all paid very
great respect to Schrodinger.

00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:13.310
Schrodinger wrote a book
called What Is Life?

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:15.470
Everybody claimed to read it.

00:29:15.470 --> 00:29:16.220
I read it.

00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:18.500
I didn't understand it.

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:21.150
So it had no influence on me.

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:24.750
But in thinking back
on it some years ago,

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:27.440
I came to the conclusion
that Schrodinger

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:31.340
had made a fundamental mistake.

00:29:31.340 --> 00:29:37.040
He said the genes contained
the program for development,

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:42.380
as we would put it this day,
and the means to execute them.

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:45.860
What von Neumann
said, they didn't

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:48.560
contain the means of execution.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:53.750
They contained the description
of the means of execution.

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:58.400
In other words, the program
is not self-reading.

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:00.920
You'll have to build
a reader for it.

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:04.610
And that's, of course, what
von Neumann's [INAUDIBLE],,

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:11.030
and without this, you can't
make a self-reproducing machine

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:15.260
because it has to transmit
to the next machine

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:18.060
a description of
the means to do it.

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:23.030
And I think that this is
the fundamental thing that

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:24.630
lies behind this.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:29.420
And so if you like,
if you want to say

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:34.010
I've got this text in
DNA, it's a long sequence,

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:35.240
can we read it?

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:39.920
Can I look in there and
say yes, that's a zebra,

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:42.540
and it's going to be
able to do these things?

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:47.570
And that is if we believe
in what we can do.

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:55.120
So when you take this, and you
try to analyze the difference,

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:59.880
analyze the relationship
between what we inherit

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:03.840
and how we can perform.

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:05.990
And the big argument
at the time,

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:09.630
that there is a connection
between genes and behavior,

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:15.220
which gave rise to a whole
lot of other problems,

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:19.440
such as saying that
intelligence is something that

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:21.840
is inherited with the genome.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:23.760
Those are not the
questions, really,

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:28.530
to ask, because I think you
must divide the problem into two

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:32.730
as, indeed, it is divided
into two in von Neumann's

00:31:32.730 --> 00:31:34.180
view of it.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:41.850
The first thing is how do
the genes specify and build

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:46.580
a machine that
performs the behavior?

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:52.010
And how does that machine
perform the behavior?

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:53.600
That is a separate question.

00:31:53.600 --> 00:31:57.560
Of course the two are
connected, as indeed they are,

00:31:57.560 --> 00:32:02.880
but they must be distinguished,
because what we are asking is,

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:04.700
if we're looking
at the behavior,

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:10.380
the behavior is represented
[? and ?] the genome [? has ?]

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:16.740
a description of how to
build a machine that behaves.

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:21.330
And you see this is very
important to get that through,

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:26.760
because the deepest problem
is how did all of this evolve?

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:30.440
Because you can only
change the description.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:34.200
So there are very
interesting questions

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:37.170
that are attached to this.

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:42.050
And in following
this line of thought,

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:46.760
I thought that the only way
to give a scientific theory

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:53.410
of a nervous system is to ask,
how does the wiring diagram,

00:32:53.410 --> 00:32:57.280
if I can call it that,
compute behavior?

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:01.220
Because if we know
how this is done,

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:04.780
we can look at the
deeper computation later,

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:10.540
which is how is the script
translated into the machinery

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:12.620
that builds this?

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:15.610
And in fact, I think
a lot of science

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:21.010
will now go to what I call
the forward question, which

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:24.820
is how do we connect
the output of a system

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:27.050
with its wiring diagram?

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:30.560
Which is the thing I
think we have to solve.

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:34.060
But of course, this
is a grand thing,

00:33:34.060 --> 00:33:35.950
and one of the
things you learn is

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:39.940
that there is a difference
between vision and eyesight.

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:42.830
I learned this by
describing a friend of mine

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:46.600
as a man of great vision
but poor eyesight.

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:50.740
So we have to have good
eyesight to implement anything

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:51.580
like this.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:54.370
And that is the
story of the worm.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:56.950
The worm has 302 neurons.

00:33:56.950 --> 00:34:00.400
Seemed to me it was
a finite problem.

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:03.640
We could come to the
end of description,

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:08.889
and then after that, we
could deal with the questions

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:11.290
that everybody
raises when you do

00:34:11.290 --> 00:34:15.850
modeling, which is the skeptic
who stands there and says how

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:18.760
do you know there's not
another wire that starts

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:21.219
in the big toe,
runs up at the back,

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:23.469
and connects this with this?

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:28.000
You have to be able to
say we know all the wires.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:30.199
Then you can proceed.

00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:37.480
So this was, then, to get to a
total analysis of the structure

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:41.469
of the nervous system,
the structure of the brain

00:34:41.469 --> 00:34:47.409
of the worm, 302 neurons, which
will he finally accomplished.

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:54.730
Took 20 years, because we had
to many, many other romances

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:57.010
with computers in between.

00:34:57.010 --> 00:35:03.340
We tried to mechanize this,
but to do this in the 1960s,

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:05.500
was impossible.

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:09.520
Nowadays, it can be done,
and there's huge activity

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:14.170
now in getting these
wiring diagrams specified.

00:35:14.170 --> 00:35:18.880
In my opinion, sometimes
overzealously applied,

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:23.170
because it's not going to
answer the question that we

00:35:23.170 --> 00:35:24.920
wanted to answer.

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:29.200
If I take a worm of exactly
the same genetic structure,

00:35:29.200 --> 00:35:33.340
will I find exactly the
same wiring diagram?

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:36.040
Today, you could
ask sure, I've just

00:35:36.040 --> 00:35:39.580
cut a section of
this mouse's brain,

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:42.010
and I've found these synapses.

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:45.730
And you say well, maybe
if you delayed your knife

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:50.290
a half an hour, would you
have found the same synapses?

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:52.340
And indeed, if you
go to another mouse

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:56.590
and look at the same cell, is
it going to be the same there?

00:35:56.590 --> 00:35:58.440
However.

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:05.430
So I think that in
approaching this question,

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:10.200
I think that we are on the
threshold of really asking

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:16.200
very serious questions, both
in biology and in relationship

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:19.600
to the behavior of
complex systems.

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:25.050
And that's why I think this
initiative is very, very

00:36:25.050 --> 00:36:27.030
important at the time--

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:29.850
important to do
now, because I think

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:36.480
we all have to take a completely
different approach to this

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:39.370
in the future.

00:36:39.370 --> 00:36:43.740
As you well know, my
colleague Francis Crick

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:47.910
got interested in
consciousness at one stage.

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:51.070
And I was asked, some time ago.

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:55.810
what did I think of
consciousness as a problem?

00:36:55.810 --> 00:36:59.490
The great physicist,
Wigner, thought

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:04.260
that consciousness would be the
explanation as why you can't

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:07.390
predict anything with
quantum mechanics,

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:11.900
that there would be
this added ingredient.

00:37:11.900 --> 00:37:15.890
My view is that I think
consciousness will never

00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:19.010
be solved, but will disappear.

00:37:19.010 --> 00:37:21.830
Now, 50 years from
now, people will

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:24.770
look back and say
what did they think

00:37:24.770 --> 00:37:27.070
they were talking about here?

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:28.100
Right?

00:37:28.100 --> 00:37:31.460
I'll tell you one other
thing that has the same thing

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:33.320
and has disappeared.

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:37.880
It is a thing called
embryological determination.

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:42.800
We had many discussions
in the '50s and '60s.

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:46.790
Is determination different
from differentiation?

00:37:46.790 --> 00:37:50.900
Are they different processes?

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:53.540
Nobody talks that way
anymore, because we

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:58.760
have a completely
different view of this.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:01.580
And this is what I
would like to say

00:38:01.580 --> 00:38:05.750
is going to be changing
those views, changing

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:11.420
many of the other views of which
I'm not equipped to talk about.

00:38:11.420 --> 00:38:14.090
But I'll tell you the
one view that I would

00:38:14.090 --> 00:38:16.910
like to see very much changed.

00:38:16.910 --> 00:38:21.890
And that is the view of
what a gene stands for.

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:26.780
And in doing this, I will
just deliver a little parable

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:28.700
in my sermon.

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:34.510
It was said that when we
sequence the human genome,

00:38:34.510 --> 00:38:37.730
and we sequence the
chimpanzee genome,

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:42.500
we will find one extra
gene in the human.

00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:44.570
That'll be the
gene for language,

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:48.302
and we'll call it
the Chomsky gene.

00:38:48.302 --> 00:38:51.870
But there's an
alternative explanation,

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:55.760
which is during the revolution,
chimpanzees discovered

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:57.350
talking gets you into trouble.

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:03.360
And so they evolved a
language suppressor gene.

00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:08.920
Then we'll call it
the Chimski gene.

00:39:08.920 --> 00:39:10.030
Thank you very much.

00:39:22.275 --> 00:39:22.900
PINKER: Marvin.

00:39:37.430 --> 00:39:38.680
MINSKY: I have too many notes.

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:48.810
When Brenner started
working on his worm,

00:39:48.810 --> 00:39:53.200
C. elegans, that
was a big project.

00:39:53.200 --> 00:39:55.650
1960, was it?

00:39:55.650 --> 00:40:01.930
And to map out the nervous
system of this animal

00:40:01.930 --> 00:40:04.950
as well as the
rest of it, you had

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:09.120
to make very small
slices with a microtome

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:16.760
and trace the nerves and
other organs in every layer.

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:20.930
And that's a tremendous
amount of work to do by hand.

00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:26.260
And he had employees, graduate
students, I don't know what,

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:28.750
and this process was
taking a long time.

00:40:31.430 --> 00:40:34.960
At the same time, McCarthy and
I had started the Artificial

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:38.110
Intelligence Group here
and had done a lot of work

00:40:38.110 --> 00:40:42.310
on computer vision,
even by 1960.

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:46.360
So I visited Brenner
and suggested

00:40:46.360 --> 00:40:50.590
that he should import a couple
of our graduate students

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:55.240
who would automate
the process of vision.

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:58.600
And Brenner said no.

00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:00.520
And I said why?

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:04.270
And he said well,
both of our fields

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:10.270
are in their very early stages,
and I have these budding

00:41:10.270 --> 00:41:14.770
genetic biologists or whatever,
and you have these budding

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:16.820
computer experts.

00:41:16.820 --> 00:41:20.710
And if you let any of them
into my lab, all of my students

00:41:20.710 --> 00:41:23.800
will realize that
your subject is

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:25.495
much more exciting and easier.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:31.210
Do you remember that?

00:41:34.570 --> 00:41:39.140
And it took me a
while to realize

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:40.520
he was absolutely right.

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:50.440
I don't know where to start.

00:41:50.440 --> 00:41:56.110
I love MIT, and I've been
here in a sense since 1957,

00:41:56.110 --> 00:42:01.480
because I started at
Harvard as an undergraduate,

00:42:01.480 --> 00:42:06.920
and I could talk about
that for two days.

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:08.950
But in the course of--

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:12.340
and then I went to graduate
school at Princeton.

00:42:12.340 --> 00:42:18.450
And I'm not sure where
I'm going with this.

00:42:18.450 --> 00:42:23.810
But every time I've got
interested in doing something,

00:42:23.810 --> 00:42:25.440
I was incredibly lucky.

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:28.190
I never had to do any real work.

00:42:28.190 --> 00:42:30.590
If I had a mathematics
problem, well,

00:42:30.590 --> 00:42:35.180
when I was a
sophomore at Harvard,

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:39.950
I sat down at a table with a
young man named Andrew Gleason

00:42:39.950 --> 00:42:43.640
who had won the Putnam--

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:47.510
first prize in the Putnam
Mathematical Competition three

00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:51.410
or maybe four years in a row,
which sort of excluded everyone

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.210
else in the world.

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:56.100
And I asked what he was doing.

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:59.660
And he said he was
trying to solve

00:42:59.660 --> 00:43:04.640
Hilbert's fifth problem, which
is that every continuous--

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:08.620
every locally continuous group--

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:11.420
oh, I forget what it is.

00:43:11.420 --> 00:43:15.620
Anyway, oh, every continuous
group is differentiable.

00:43:15.620 --> 00:43:17.610
It's a very nice problem.

00:43:17.610 --> 00:43:19.370
And I said, how
long do you think

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:21.830
it will take to solve that?

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:24.590
And he said nine years.

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:30.290
I had never met anyone with
a plan of that magnitude

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:32.940
only a few years older than me.

00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:35.600
And I asked what that was,
and he said well, first I'll

00:43:35.600 --> 00:43:38.630
have to do Dedekind cuts
to show that the thing has

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:41.120
to have real numbers
and blah, blah, blah.

00:43:41.120 --> 00:43:44.970
Anyway, it took him eight years.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:52.090
And I think, somehow I was very
early in my research career,

00:43:52.090 --> 00:43:55.260
and it seemed to me that
eight years was plausible,

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:57.620
let's do things.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:43:59.520
Anyway, I was just very lucky.

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:02.820
I ran into von
Neumann at some point

00:44:02.820 --> 00:44:05.620
when I was doing my
thesis at Princeton,

00:44:05.620 --> 00:44:07.830
which was on neural networks.

00:44:07.830 --> 00:44:12.270
And I got the idea of doing that
from earlier work by McCulloch

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:19.000
and lots of people in the 1940s.

00:44:19.000 --> 00:44:22.510
It turned out that in
1895, Sigmund Freud

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:26.230
had written a little
paper on neural networks,

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:27.700
but no one would publish it.

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:32.200
And it wasn't published
till around 1950.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:34.120
Well, I guess the
same question came up,

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:38.230
because I was in the math
department at Princeton

00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:45.400
and they asked von Neumann is
this PhD thesis mathematics?

00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:49.720
And I'm happy to say that
he replied if it isn't now,

00:44:49.720 --> 00:44:52.690
it soon will be.

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:58.090
And anyway, there was
something great about the era,

00:44:58.090 --> 00:45:03.070
and it's hard to talk about how
things started because it was

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:06.700
so different from it is today.

00:45:06.700 --> 00:45:11.320
See, 1950 is shortly
after World War II,

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:14.410
and the country was
very prosperous,

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:17.020
and there were lots of
research institutions,

00:45:17.020 --> 00:45:19.580
because there were
a lot of monopolies.

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:23.170
I mean, a company like General
Electric, or Westinghouse,

00:45:23.170 --> 00:45:26.890
or even CBS were pretty huge.

00:45:26.890 --> 00:45:28.960
And they-- and Bell
Laboratories, of course,

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:30.020
was a monopoly.

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:32.680
The others only acted like it.

00:45:32.680 --> 00:45:36.670
And so they would start
out different projects.

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:40.120
And one, I guess
John McCarthy and I

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:45.250
in the summer of 1951 or two--

00:45:45.250 --> 00:45:50.260
anyway, we got summer
jobs at Bell Labs

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:53.410
and someone told us not to
work on any problem that

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:54.925
would take less than 30 years.

00:45:57.590 --> 00:46:00.880
They didn't have the
tenure deadline either.

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:07.450
Now to get tenure you have to
be quick, because legally, I

00:46:07.450 --> 00:46:12.740
think tenure is seven years, but
they like it to be six years,

00:46:12.740 --> 00:46:15.610
because you don't want
to keep people hanging.

00:46:15.610 --> 00:46:18.730
And what's more, if you
fire somebody before that,

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:22.360
you have to pay about
a year termination fee

00:46:22.360 --> 00:46:26.030
and oh well, blah, blah.

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:28.630
So it's very hard to
get a research grant

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:31.480
for five years, which
was sort of the standard

00:46:31.480 --> 00:46:35.920
that NIH and many of
these laboratories had.

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:39.520
So any questions?

00:46:46.052 --> 00:46:48.010
Anyway, we started this
artificial Intelligence

00:46:48.010 --> 00:46:49.950
laboratory.

00:46:49.950 --> 00:46:51.345
And the first few--

00:46:54.100 --> 00:46:56.090
I'll just stop in the
middle of the sentence

00:46:56.090 --> 00:46:59.485
when time is up, because
I never make plans.

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:03.198
I could talk--

00:47:03.198 --> 00:47:03.740
PINKER: What?

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:08.880
MINSKY: Oh, it's an iPhone.

00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:16.370
I have one.

00:47:16.370 --> 00:47:27.530
Mine is [? pink. ?] Anyway,
how did all this happen?

00:47:27.530 --> 00:47:31.190
When I was a child, there were
a lot of books in the house,

00:47:31.190 --> 00:47:37.260
and I got interested
in reading them.

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:41.100
And it seemed to me that
they fell into two classes.

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:47.700
One was fiction,
and that was novels.

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:50.850
This is a joke I've
made many times.

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:54.030
And I read a few novels,
and it seemed to me

00:47:54.030 --> 00:47:56.990
that they were
all the same, even

00:47:56.990 --> 00:48:01.220
by great writers from
Shakespeare on down.

00:48:01.220 --> 00:48:07.370
And there are about six or
seven sins, or the major ways

00:48:07.370 --> 00:48:09.750
that people screw
up their lives.

00:48:09.750 --> 00:48:13.910
And so a typical
piece of fiction

00:48:13.910 --> 00:48:17.310
describes a situation
where something goes wrong,

00:48:17.310 --> 00:48:21.050
and you try to fix it, and
then something else goes wrong.

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:25.270
And then this goes on until
finally you cure them all

00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:27.830
or you die.

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:31.780
It doesn't make much difference.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:35.490
So that's what general
literature is about.

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:40.030
And science fiction is
about everything else.

00:48:40.030 --> 00:48:44.010
So somehow, Isaac
Asimov lived nearby

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:45.880
when he had just finished.

00:48:45.880 --> 00:48:50.430
And just as I ran out of the
Jules Verne, and HG Wells,

00:48:50.430 --> 00:48:55.110
and Aldous Huxley, and John
Campbell, and the early science

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:58.800
fiction writers,
exactly the right people

00:48:58.800 --> 00:49:04.710
started to turn up, which
was Robert Heinlein in 1942,

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:08.190
I guess, and Isaac
Asimov a little later,

00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:11.160
and Frederick Poe.

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:14.700
I met Isaac because he lived
in Newton, which is nearby,

00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:19.650
and at this time, or
somewhat later anyway, we

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:21.870
were building the first robots.

00:49:21.870 --> 00:49:25.230
Pretty much the-- although
a guy named Grey Walter,

00:49:25.230 --> 00:49:30.450
neurologist in the
west of England

00:49:30.450 --> 00:49:33.570
had made some interesting ones.

00:49:33.570 --> 00:49:41.400
And I invited Asimov to come and
see our robots, and he refused.

00:49:41.400 --> 00:49:42.750
And this happened a few times.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:45.010
And finally I said why not?

00:49:45.010 --> 00:49:47.760
And he said I'm imagining
really intelligent robots

00:49:47.760 --> 00:49:50.110
of the future.

00:49:50.110 --> 00:49:51.900
And if I came and--

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:53.895
I'm sure that your
robots are really stupid.

00:49:57.880 --> 00:49:59.410
Which they were.

00:49:59.410 --> 00:50:02.560
They could play a very
slow game of ping-pong

00:50:02.560 --> 00:50:05.440
and a few things like that.

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:07.840
You might wonder why
aren't there any robots

00:50:07.840 --> 00:50:16.010
that you can send in to fix the
Japanese reactor that's broken?

00:50:16.010 --> 00:50:18.350
Reactors, I should say.

00:50:18.350 --> 00:50:21.090
The answer is that
for the last--

00:50:21.090 --> 00:50:27.380
there was a lot of progress in
robotics in the 1960s and '70s,

00:50:27.380 --> 00:50:30.500
then something went wrong.

00:50:30.500 --> 00:50:34.790
If you go to Carnegie Mellon,
I don't want to mention names,

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:39.140
or anywhere else,
you'll find students

00:50:39.140 --> 00:50:43.940
are slaving over robots that
play basketball, or soccer,

00:50:43.940 --> 00:50:48.260
or dance, or make
funny faces at you,

00:50:48.260 --> 00:50:50.180
and they're not
making them smarter.

00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:57.410
They don't use or don't realize
that they need something

00:50:57.410 --> 00:50:59.630
like artificial intelligence.

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:04.400
So I'd like to see a return
back to the old days.

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:07.460
Brenner mentioned
consciousness I think,

00:51:07.460 --> 00:51:12.090
and I agree entirely that
it's a very bad word.

00:51:12.090 --> 00:51:16.250
Because if you look at my
recent book, which is badly

00:51:16.250 --> 00:51:20.670
named The Motion
Machine, it says

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:26.240
notice that people use the
word consciousness for 35 very

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:28.670
different things, like
remembering what you've

00:51:28.670 --> 00:51:32.720
recently done, or making
certain kinds of choices,

00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:36.110
or reacting to certain
stimuli, and so forth.

00:51:36.110 --> 00:51:41.480
And so virtually everything we
don't understand about the mind

00:51:41.480 --> 00:51:45.200
when people do things is
attributed to this word.

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:52.040
So anyway, that's
one of the problems.

00:51:52.040 --> 00:51:56.030
And yet I see that professional
people in various sciences

00:51:56.030 --> 00:52:00.000
still use the distinction
between conscious and not.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:04.790
And as far as I can see, you
can't get rid of that word.

00:52:04.790 --> 00:52:06.470
What's its main use?

00:52:06.470 --> 00:52:10.250
Its main use is deciding
when someone is a criminal.

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:13.610
If they run over you by
accident, well, that's very bad

00:52:13.610 --> 00:52:15.800
and you might lose your license.

00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:18.020
If you run over
someone on purpose,

00:52:18.020 --> 00:52:21.670
that's intentional,
conscious, blah, blah, blah.

00:52:21.670 --> 00:52:24.680
And so we have this strange
situation in psychology

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:27.560
that I don't see in
other sciences which

00:52:27.560 --> 00:52:31.490
is to use words that have
been around for a long time

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:34.270
without--

00:52:34.270 --> 00:52:37.670
I'm down on the 10%
of my notes, so.

00:52:50.920 --> 00:52:51.670
PINKER: Thank you.

00:52:51.670 --> 00:52:52.212
Noam Chomsky.

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:09.150
CHOMSKY: Well, I kind of like
that idea of the language

00:53:09.150 --> 00:53:11.375
suppressor gene.

00:53:11.375 --> 00:53:15.840
That actually has a venerable
history, which maybe you know.

00:53:15.840 --> 00:53:23.100
In the 17th century, when
Westerners were discovering

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:28.950
all kinds of exotic creatures
like apes, and [INAUDIBLE],,

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:31.500
and others, and they
weren't sure which

00:53:31.500 --> 00:53:34.950
was which, there was
a lot of discussion

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:39.050
about whether that
ape, orangutans,

00:53:39.050 --> 00:53:42.030
you know, apes can speak.

00:53:42.030 --> 00:53:44.640
They didn't seem to, and there
was a lot of debate about why

00:53:44.640 --> 00:53:45.810
they didn't.

00:53:45.810 --> 00:53:48.630
And there was a proposal
that there was--

00:53:48.630 --> 00:53:50.470
they didn't call it
genes in those days,

00:53:50.470 --> 00:53:54.520
but there was the analog of
a language suppressor gene.

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:58.200
Louis Racine, who was the
son of the famous dramatist,

00:53:58.200 --> 00:54:03.090
suggested that apes are really
more intelligent than humans,

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:06.150
and the proof of it is
that they don't speak.

00:54:06.150 --> 00:54:08.340
Because they knew that
if they did speak,

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:09.960
we would enslave them.

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:11.060
So they keep quiet.

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:17.670
This, incidentally,
led to the conclusion

00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:21.735
that a brilliant father
can't have a brilliant son.

00:54:26.520 --> 00:54:31.830
I was in our [? early ?]
Building 20 in the golden age.

00:54:31.830 --> 00:54:33.540
And it actually
was a golden age.

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:36.930
But I got here in 1955.

00:54:36.930 --> 00:54:39.780
It was quite an exciting place.

00:54:39.780 --> 00:54:43.160
A lot of enthusiasm, innovation.

00:54:43.160 --> 00:54:45.510
A very lively
interaction among people

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:49.570
of widely different fields.

00:54:49.570 --> 00:54:53.720
It's a kind of
community which I think

00:54:53.720 --> 00:54:58.770
will be very hard, if even
possible, to reconstruct.

00:54:58.770 --> 00:55:01.510
I also have many very
warm memories of it.

00:55:01.510 --> 00:55:04.500
In fact, some of them
very hot memories.

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:07.260
Now those of you who
were around in those days

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:09.610
may remember that
over the summer,

00:55:09.610 --> 00:55:13.530
Building 20 was unbearably hot.

00:55:13.530 --> 00:55:15.930
You could barely survive.

00:55:15.930 --> 00:55:18.870
Morris Halle and I then
shared a small office

00:55:18.870 --> 00:55:23.370
and we decided we
would try to get

00:55:23.370 --> 00:55:29.220
the Institute to put in and
air conditioner, a window air

00:55:29.220 --> 00:55:30.570
conditioner.

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:33.960
So we sent a message up
through the bureaucracy,

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:37.870
and it finally reached
whatever high office it got to,

00:55:37.870 --> 00:55:40.990
and something came
back down to us

00:55:40.990 --> 00:55:44.970
finally with a message saying
you can't put in an air

00:55:44.970 --> 00:55:47.640
conditioner because it
wouldn't be consistent

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:51.490
with the decor of Building 20.

00:55:51.490 --> 00:55:54.840
Those of you who have ever
seen a picture of Building 20

00:55:54.840 --> 00:55:56.760
would know what this meant.

00:55:56.760 --> 00:56:01.470
Fortunately, we could find a
friendly janitor who for $10

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:03.960
was willing to break
the rules and we were

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:06.360
able to survive the summer.

00:56:06.360 --> 00:56:10.440
Back a few years earlier,
in fact, going back

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:14.010
to the time of the famous
paper, Alan Turing's

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:18.920
paper on machine
intelligence in 1950.

00:56:18.920 --> 00:56:21.090
Early '50s, there
was a small group

00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:24.660
of graduate students
down the road

00:56:24.660 --> 00:56:31.770
who were dissatisfied with the
prevailing conceptions of how

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:38.770
to understand thought,
behavior, language, and so on.

00:56:38.770 --> 00:56:43.170
And we were trying to
see if we can construct

00:56:43.170 --> 00:56:46.900
another way of looking
at these topics, which

00:56:46.900 --> 00:56:52.500
might be more promising
and integrated better

00:56:52.500 --> 00:56:54.750
into the general sciences.

00:56:54.750 --> 00:57:00.000
And Turing's comments
had a certain resonance.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:02.730
You may recall that
in this paper, which

00:57:02.730 --> 00:57:04.590
is about machine
intelligence, he

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:08.460
begins by saying that the
question whether machines

00:57:08.460 --> 00:57:12.810
can think is too meaningless
to deserve discussion.

00:57:12.810 --> 00:57:16.230
He didn't explain
why, but he presumably

00:57:16.230 --> 00:57:19.800
meant that it's a question
of what kind of metaphor

00:57:19.800 --> 00:57:22.120
you are willing to accept.

00:57:22.120 --> 00:57:26.070
So it's like asking do
airplanes really fly?

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:28.300
Or do submarines really swim?

00:57:28.300 --> 00:57:30.710
If you want to extend
the metaphor, yeah.

00:57:30.710 --> 00:57:31.470
If not, no.

00:57:31.470 --> 00:57:33.480
But it's not a factual question.

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:35.070
He nevertheless went
on to say that it

00:57:35.070 --> 00:57:41.130
would be a very good idea
to construct hard problems

00:57:41.130 --> 00:57:43.680
to see if you can
design machines,

00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:46.860
meaning hardware and
software, to solve them.

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:49.350
And the famous
proposal of his was

00:57:49.350 --> 00:57:51.000
what he called his
Imitation Game,

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:54.180
later came to be
called the Turing Test.

00:57:54.180 --> 00:57:56.370
He thought that
might be an incentive

00:57:56.370 --> 00:58:00.330
to develop better
machines, better software.

00:58:00.330 --> 00:58:02.500
And for that reason
it's good to do it,

00:58:02.500 --> 00:58:04.680
but we're not asking
do machines think?

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:07.600
He also suggested that
maybe in the course of time,

00:58:07.600 --> 00:58:10.500
he said 50 years,
people will come--

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:12.120
because of this
work, people will

00:58:12.120 --> 00:58:16.200
come to think about
thinking in a different way.

00:58:16.200 --> 00:58:18.920
You can ask whether
that's happened.

00:58:18.920 --> 00:58:21.780
The machines-- it
certainly was an incentive

00:58:21.780 --> 00:58:24.090
to develop better machines.

00:58:24.090 --> 00:58:28.020
And that had a certain
analogy to the kinds of things

00:58:28.020 --> 00:58:29.830
we were concerned with.

00:58:29.830 --> 00:58:33.320
There is for one
thing, because when

00:58:33.320 --> 00:58:37.290
you look at language
seriously, it wasn't really

00:58:37.290 --> 00:58:39.120
done at that time,
unfortunately.

00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:41.580
You can see right
off that it's--

00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:44.610
what each of us has
internalized is some kind

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:46.680
of computational system.

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:53.220
A system which, in fact,
determines an unbounded array

00:58:53.220 --> 00:58:56.670
of structured
expressions, each of which

00:58:56.670 --> 00:59:00.540
has a dual interpretation
[? that's ?] interpreted

00:59:00.540 --> 00:59:04.740
by the sensory motor system as
some kind of externalization,

00:59:04.740 --> 00:59:08.740
sound, or we know
no other modalities,

00:59:08.740 --> 00:59:13.560
and it's interpreted at a
internal, roughly speaking,

00:59:13.560 --> 00:59:15.630
kind of a thought
system, called sometimes

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:19.170
the conceptual
intentional system,

00:59:19.170 --> 00:59:24.390
as having a specific meaning,
which then enters into planning

00:59:24.390 --> 00:59:26.140
of action and so on.

00:59:26.140 --> 00:59:27.870
So that's a
computational system.

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:31.740
And the constant understanding
of computational systems

00:59:31.740 --> 00:59:35.400
had advanced quite considerably
by the mid-20th century,

00:59:35.400 --> 00:59:38.400
in large part because
of Turing's work.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:41.850
And that sort of fit naturally.

00:59:41.850 --> 00:59:47.040
Also, his provisos
made some sense for us.

00:59:47.040 --> 00:59:51.760
There is a standard question,
what counts as a language?

00:59:51.760 --> 00:59:55.770
And I think Turing's
response was accurate.

00:59:55.770 --> 00:59:57.600
The question's too
meaningless to deserve

00:59:57.600 --> 01:00:02.670
discussion for the same
reasons as machines thinking.

01:00:02.670 --> 01:00:05.490
If you want to call
the communication

01:00:05.490 --> 01:00:10.170
systems of hundreds of species
of bees language, okay,

01:00:10.170 --> 01:00:11.520
you can call it that.

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:14.880
That means you're accepting
a certain metaphor.

01:00:14.880 --> 01:00:17.990
If you don't want to
call it that, don't.

01:00:17.990 --> 01:00:19.980
It's not a factual question.

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:23.850
The facts of the matter are
these systems differ radically.

01:00:23.850 --> 01:00:26.610
Every animal
communication system known

01:00:26.610 --> 01:00:30.380
differs radically in
its basic principles

01:00:30.380 --> 01:00:33.300
and structural principles
of use [? others ?]

01:00:33.300 --> 01:00:35.070
from human language.

01:00:35.070 --> 01:00:37.150
And human language is,
in fact, [? here ?]

01:00:37.150 --> 01:00:39.450
it's different
from Turing's case.

01:00:39.450 --> 01:00:44.460
It is, in fact, a particular
biological system, one

01:00:44.460 --> 01:00:47.100
that each of us has
internalized, that,

01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:50.190
at its core, a computational
system with the kind

01:00:50.190 --> 01:00:52.800
of properties that I mentioned.

01:00:52.800 --> 01:00:57.300
And there's no problem
here about constructing

01:00:57.300 --> 01:01:00.600
hard problems to deal with,
because it was quickly

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:02.400
discovered, as
soon as the effort

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:07.710
to construct such computational
systems was undertaken,

01:01:07.710 --> 01:01:09.840
that almost nothing
was understood.

01:01:09.840 --> 01:01:12.330
Everything you looked
at was a puzzle.

01:01:12.330 --> 01:01:15.900
And the more you learn, the
more puzzles you discover.

01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:17.610
That was kind of
surprising at the time,

01:01:17.610 --> 01:01:20.850
because it was assumed,
the prevailing conception

01:01:20.850 --> 01:01:24.090
was that everything
was more or less known.

01:01:24.090 --> 01:01:28.110
There aren't any real
problems about language.

01:01:28.110 --> 01:01:29.715
It's just a matter of--

01:01:29.715 --> 01:01:33.600
well, a famous version
was [INAUDIBLE],,

01:01:33.600 --> 01:01:36.780
a highly influential
philosopher, most influential,

01:01:36.780 --> 01:01:40.560
whose picture was
that language is just

01:01:40.560 --> 01:01:45.040
a collection of sentences
associated with one another

01:01:45.040 --> 01:01:47.070
and with stimuli
by the mechanism

01:01:47.070 --> 01:01:48.930
of conditioned response.

01:01:48.930 --> 01:01:51.270
So all there is to
do is just trace

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:53.520
the individual
cases of histories

01:01:53.520 --> 01:01:56.460
of conditioned response
and you get the answers.

01:01:56.460 --> 01:01:59.280
Among linguists,
it was very widely

01:01:59.280 --> 01:02:02.460
assumed that
languages can differ

01:02:02.460 --> 01:02:07.770
in virtually any possible way
with virtually no constraints.

01:02:07.770 --> 01:02:10.600
And the only problem
in linguistics,

01:02:10.600 --> 01:02:12.750
I remember this
as a student, was

01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:16.440
to collect more data from a
wider variety of languages

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:22.330
and apply to them the techniques
of analysis which were assumed

01:02:22.330 --> 01:02:24.340
to be more or less understood.

01:02:24.340 --> 01:02:25.950
And that's the field.

01:02:25.950 --> 01:02:27.870
But nothing could be puzzling.

01:02:27.870 --> 01:02:29.820
On the other hand,
as soon as the effort

01:02:29.820 --> 01:02:35.550
was undertaken to construct
an actual computational system

01:02:35.550 --> 01:02:38.520
with the properties
that I mentioned,

01:02:38.520 --> 01:02:40.980
it was discovered that
everything is a puzzle.

01:02:40.980 --> 01:02:42.630
Nothing is understood.

01:02:42.630 --> 01:02:45.390
And some of the puzzles are
quite simple, and some of them

01:02:45.390 --> 01:02:47.460
are still outstanding, in fact.

01:02:47.460 --> 01:02:52.710
So just to be concrete,
take the simple sentence

01:02:52.710 --> 01:02:55.870
can eagles that fly swim?

01:02:55.870 --> 01:02:56.370
Okay.

01:02:56.370 --> 01:02:59.700
We understand that it means
we're asking a question about

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:01.110
whether they can swim.

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:04.800
We're not asking a question
about whether they can fly.

01:03:04.800 --> 01:03:07.070
But that's a puzzle.

01:03:07.070 --> 01:03:11.090
Why doesn't the "can"
relate to the closest verb?

01:03:11.090 --> 01:03:13.970
Why does it relate to
the more remote verb?

01:03:13.970 --> 01:03:16.910
One which, in fact, is
closest in a different sense.

01:03:16.910 --> 01:03:18.960
It's structurally closest.

01:03:18.960 --> 01:03:24.590
So similarly, we can say are
eagles that fly swimming?

01:03:24.590 --> 01:03:28.700
But we can't say are
eagles that flying swim?

01:03:28.700 --> 01:03:31.010
Now that's a reasonable thought.

01:03:31.010 --> 01:03:35.690
It's asking is it the case that
eagles that are flying swim?

01:03:35.690 --> 01:03:37.850
But somehow we can't say it.

01:03:37.850 --> 01:03:39.650
The design of
language prevents us

01:03:39.650 --> 01:03:43.280
from articulating a
perfectly fine thought

01:03:43.280 --> 01:03:45.830
except by paraphrases.

01:03:45.830 --> 01:03:49.310
And there is a question
why that should be true.

01:03:49.310 --> 01:03:52.310
From a computational
point of view,

01:03:52.310 --> 01:03:59.270
counting computing the closest
verb, computing linear order

01:03:59.270 --> 01:04:02.360
and linear closeness,
is far simpler

01:04:02.360 --> 01:04:05.030
than computing structural
closeness, which

01:04:05.030 --> 01:04:07.670
requires complex assumptions
about the structure

01:04:07.670 --> 01:04:08.820
of the object.

01:04:08.820 --> 01:04:10.740
So there's a puzzle.

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:14.600
And that puzzle had been
around for thousands of years,

01:04:14.600 --> 01:04:17.070
except nobody thought
it was a puzzle.

01:04:17.070 --> 01:04:20.540
It's one of [? really ?]
many such cases.

01:04:20.540 --> 01:04:23.570
I should mention that in
the last couple of years,

01:04:23.570 --> 01:04:26.180
sort of a substantial
industry has

01:04:26.180 --> 01:04:29.625
developed to try to deal with
it on computational grounds.

01:04:29.625 --> 01:04:30.950
And I won't talk about that.

01:04:30.950 --> 01:04:32.590
I don't think it gets anywhere.

01:04:32.590 --> 01:04:34.070
But it's a real puzzle.

01:04:34.070 --> 01:04:38.540
And furthermore, that
principle of using

01:04:38.540 --> 01:04:42.530
structural distance
but not linear distance

01:04:42.530 --> 01:04:44.340
is all over the place.

01:04:44.340 --> 01:04:48.650
You find it in structure after
structure in all languages,

01:04:48.650 --> 01:04:51.710
and it seems to just be some
universal property of language,

01:04:51.710 --> 01:04:53.690
and a very puzzling one.

01:04:53.690 --> 01:04:55.730
Well, discoveries
of that kind are

01:04:55.730 --> 01:04:58.970
kind of reminiscent
of the earliest

01:04:58.970 --> 01:05:02.750
moments of modern science.

01:05:02.750 --> 01:05:05.060
Go back to say 1600.

01:05:05.060 --> 01:05:08.870
For thousands of
years, scientists

01:05:08.870 --> 01:05:14.000
had been quite satisfied with
an explanation for such things

01:05:14.000 --> 01:05:19.250
as why stones fall
down and steam goes up.

01:05:19.250 --> 01:05:22.060
They're seeking
their natural place.

01:05:22.060 --> 01:05:23.780
End of that story.

01:05:23.780 --> 01:05:26.720
When Galileo and a
few others allowed

01:05:26.720 --> 01:05:29.090
themselves to be
puzzled about that,

01:05:29.090 --> 01:05:31.170
you start getting
modern science.

01:05:31.170 --> 01:05:32.330
And I think that continues.

01:05:32.330 --> 01:05:36.260
The capacity to be puzzled
about what looks sort of obvious

01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:39.770
is a good property to cultivate.

01:05:39.770 --> 01:05:44.130
And it's an awakening, and
that began at that time.

01:05:44.130 --> 01:05:48.650
And it turns out there's
enormous linguistic evidence

01:05:48.650 --> 01:05:50.210
that this is the
way things work.

01:05:50.210 --> 01:05:53.960
There's also, in recent
years, some evidence

01:05:53.960 --> 01:05:55.760
from the neurosciences.

01:05:55.760 --> 01:05:58.550
There's some interesting
investigations

01:05:58.550 --> 01:06:02.580
being carried out by a
very good group at Milan.

01:06:02.580 --> 01:06:05.870
The linguist in the group, Andre
[? Amaro ?] is well-known here.

01:06:05.870 --> 01:06:08.300
He's been here many times.

01:06:08.300 --> 01:06:12.680
They've been investigating
the reaction of people

01:06:12.680 --> 01:06:16.010
to two types of stimuli.

01:06:16.010 --> 01:06:19.790
One all new to them.

01:06:19.790 --> 01:06:23.240
[? Oral ?] systems that
meet the conditions,

01:06:23.240 --> 01:06:25.100
the basic conditions
of what's called

01:06:25.100 --> 01:06:28.760
universal grammar of the
general principles of language,

01:06:28.760 --> 01:06:32.660
and others that violate these
conditions and use things

01:06:32.660 --> 01:06:34.290
like linear order.

01:06:34.290 --> 01:06:36.110
So for example, an
invented language

01:06:36.110 --> 01:06:40.850
in which to negate a sentence,
you take the negative word

01:06:40.850 --> 01:06:43.490
and you put it in the third
position in the sentence, let's

01:06:43.490 --> 01:06:44.060
say.

01:06:44.060 --> 01:06:45.920
And it turns out that
brain activity is

01:06:45.920 --> 01:06:47.870
quite different in these cases.

01:06:47.870 --> 01:06:50.960
In the case of a normal
language, which people

01:06:50.960 --> 01:06:54.560
have never been exposed to,
Broca's area, language areas,

01:06:54.560 --> 01:06:56.810
are activated in the normal way.

01:06:56.810 --> 01:06:59.690
But in the case of
linear order, it's not.

01:06:59.690 --> 01:07:03.010
The language areas don't
have the normal activation.

01:07:03.010 --> 01:07:06.170
Now, people may figure it out,
but they're figuring it out

01:07:06.170 --> 01:07:08.390
as some kind of
puzzle to solve, not

01:07:08.390 --> 01:07:11.060
using their
linguistic capacities.

01:07:11.060 --> 01:07:15.300
Well, all of this ought
to be puzzling, and it is.

01:07:15.300 --> 01:07:19.880
And in fact, as far as I know,
the only serious proposal

01:07:19.880 --> 01:07:24.200
as to how to explain it
has pretty far-reaching

01:07:24.200 --> 01:07:27.150
conclusions.

01:07:27.150 --> 01:07:29.720
The natural assumption
is that at the point

01:07:29.720 --> 01:07:32.900
at which the computations are
taking place in the brain,

01:07:32.900 --> 01:07:35.020
there just is no order.

01:07:35.020 --> 01:07:37.571
There's no ordering at all.

01:07:37.571 --> 01:07:39.630
All there is is hierarchy.

01:07:39.630 --> 01:07:42.320
So the only thing
the brain can compute

01:07:42.320 --> 01:07:47.300
is minimal structural
distance, because there's

01:07:47.300 --> 01:07:48.770
no linear order.

01:07:48.770 --> 01:07:51.740
Well, that looks pretty
plausible, in fact,

01:07:51.740 --> 01:07:53.630
from many points of view.

01:07:53.630 --> 01:07:57.410
One reason is that if you look
at the syntax and semantics

01:07:57.410 --> 01:08:04.370
of language, for a very
broad core class of cases,

01:08:04.370 --> 01:08:06.190
order doesn't matter.

01:08:06.190 --> 01:08:08.045
What matters is hierarchy.

01:08:08.045 --> 01:08:09.420
Furthermore, if
you look at the--

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:13.040
so that suggests that one
of those two interfaces,

01:08:13.040 --> 01:08:15.660
the semantic interface
with the thought system,

01:08:15.660 --> 01:08:18.779
just doesn't care about order.

01:08:18.779 --> 01:08:21.450
On the other hand, the
sensory motor system

01:08:21.450 --> 01:08:25.140
must have some kind of
arrangement depending on what

01:08:25.140 --> 01:08:27.300
the form of externalization is.

01:08:27.300 --> 01:08:29.529
If it's speech, it'll
be linear order.

01:08:29.529 --> 01:08:32.819
If it's signed, it'll be
various things in parallel.

01:08:32.819 --> 01:08:35.490
But it has to have
some kind of ordering.

01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:40.740
So it's reasonable to suppose
that the ordering is simply

01:08:40.740 --> 01:08:44.910
a reflex of the
externalization, but doesn't

01:08:44.910 --> 01:08:48.779
enter into the core
properties of language,

01:08:48.779 --> 01:08:52.020
which simply give you
the thought, what's

01:08:52.020 --> 01:08:53.700
used in the thought system.

01:08:53.700 --> 01:08:58.109
Now that effectively
means, if it's correct,

01:08:58.109 --> 01:09:03.750
and it seems to be, that in
the informal sense of the term

01:09:03.750 --> 01:09:05.819
design, you know,
[? not ?] designer,

01:09:05.819 --> 01:09:08.660
but in the informal
sense of the term design,

01:09:08.660 --> 01:09:13.649
language is designed for thought
and not for externalization.

01:09:13.649 --> 01:09:18.100
Externalization is
an ancillary process.

01:09:18.100 --> 01:09:19.740
And that would
mean, incidentally,

01:09:19.740 --> 01:09:21.870
that communication,
which is just

01:09:21.870 --> 01:09:24.060
a special case of
externalization,

01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:28.470
is an even more ancillary
process, contrary to what's

01:09:28.470 --> 01:09:30.600
widely believed.

01:09:30.600 --> 01:09:33.330
That has significant
implications

01:09:33.330 --> 01:09:36.430
for the study of
evolution of language.

01:09:36.430 --> 01:09:38.760
I won't go into them, but
you can think of through.

01:09:38.760 --> 01:09:41.385
And for the nature of
design of language,

01:09:41.385 --> 01:09:43.260
where [? there are ?]
other kinds of evidence

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:44.700
that have similar--

01:09:44.700 --> 01:09:47.199
that lead to
similar conclusions.

01:09:47.199 --> 01:09:49.590
It says a lot about the
architecture of mind,

01:09:49.590 --> 01:09:51.840
if this is correct.

01:09:51.840 --> 01:09:55.470
For example, one
class of cases is

01:09:55.470 --> 01:09:59.160
kind of a ubiquitous phenomena
in language, sometimes

01:09:59.160 --> 01:10:00.600
called displacement.

01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:02.490
You hear something
in one position,

01:10:02.490 --> 01:10:04.750
but you interpret
it somewhere else.

01:10:04.750 --> 01:10:08.370
So if you say what did John see?

01:10:08.370 --> 01:10:11.740
You have to interpret the
word "what" in two positions.

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:15.640
And where it is, where it's
kind of an operator asking--

01:10:15.640 --> 01:10:18.390
telling you-- asking some
question about what person

01:10:18.390 --> 01:10:20.650
or something like that.

01:10:20.650 --> 01:10:23.820
And also in another position
as the object of see.

01:10:23.820 --> 01:10:29.130
Just as in John saw
the book, or something,

01:10:29.130 --> 01:10:34.920
where it's getting its semantic
role as the object of the verb.

01:10:34.920 --> 01:10:37.200
It's got to be interpreted
in those positions.

01:10:37.200 --> 01:10:42.240
It's as if the mind is hearing
something like for which

01:10:42.240 --> 01:10:47.400
thing X, John saw X, where
the "for which" thing

01:10:47.400 --> 01:10:51.300
is an operator ranging
over the variable.

01:10:51.300 --> 01:10:53.460
That shows up in other ways.

01:10:53.460 --> 01:10:56.520
That unpronounced
element turns out

01:10:56.520 --> 01:11:00.240
to really be there for
the mental processes that

01:11:00.240 --> 01:11:01.560
are taking place.

01:11:01.560 --> 01:11:06.300
You can see that in
sentences like say,

01:11:06.300 --> 01:11:11.100
they expect to see each other,
where the phrase "each other"

01:11:11.100 --> 01:11:12.960
is referring back to they.

01:11:12.960 --> 01:11:15.520
Say the men expect
to see each other.

01:11:15.520 --> 01:11:19.617
On the other hand, if you
had a another noun in between

01:11:19.617 --> 01:11:21.630
in the right position
it wouldn't work.

01:11:21.630 --> 01:11:25.020
So if you say, the men expect
Mary to see each other,

01:11:25.020 --> 01:11:26.760
then that somehow breaks it up.

01:11:26.760 --> 01:11:29.070
Each other can't
go back to the men.

01:11:29.070 --> 01:11:33.480
Well what about who do the
men expect to see each other?

01:11:33.480 --> 01:11:36.720
Well, that's like the men
expect John to see each other.

01:11:36.720 --> 01:11:39.000
Each other doesn't
go back to the men.

01:11:39.000 --> 01:11:42.930
But there's nothing there
in the form that you hear.

01:11:42.930 --> 01:11:44.490
It's just like they expect--

01:11:44.490 --> 01:11:46.350
the men expect to
see each other.

01:11:46.350 --> 01:11:50.970
Well, that suggests strongly
that the mind is actually

01:11:50.970 --> 01:11:58.170
hearing, interpreting
for which person X,

01:11:58.170 --> 01:12:01.830
the men expect X
to see each other.

01:12:01.830 --> 01:12:05.610
And that X is like John
when you pronounce it.

01:12:05.610 --> 01:12:08.530
And things like that can
get pretty complicated.

01:12:08.530 --> 01:12:14.500
So if you take a sentence
like say, they expect--

01:12:14.500 --> 01:12:21.920
they think that every artist
likes one of his pictures best.

01:12:21.920 --> 01:12:24.720
Like maybe the first
one he painted.

01:12:24.720 --> 01:12:29.550
If you take one of
his pictures and you

01:12:29.550 --> 01:12:33.510
ask a question about it, so
which one of his pictures do

01:12:33.510 --> 01:12:37.590
they expect every
artist to like best?

01:12:37.590 --> 01:12:40.690
The answer would be,
well, his first one,

01:12:40.690 --> 01:12:43.080
even though which
of his pictures

01:12:43.080 --> 01:12:46.920
is not in the right
position to be

01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:50.660
bound by the quantifier every.

01:12:50.660 --> 01:12:53.310
And that becomes clearer if
you make a different question.

01:12:53.310 --> 01:12:55.740
Suppose you say,
which of his pictures

01:12:55.740 --> 01:13:00.520
convinced the museum that
every artist paints flowers?

01:13:00.520 --> 01:13:02.880
Well, there's no relation
in every and his there,

01:13:02.880 --> 01:13:04.410
though it's
structurally think it

01:13:04.410 --> 01:13:06.690
through about the same
as the one that works.

01:13:06.690 --> 01:13:09.300
There's really only one
plausible interpretation

01:13:09.300 --> 01:13:12.900
for that, and that is that the
phrase which of his pictures

01:13:12.900 --> 01:13:14.760
is actually appearing twice.

01:13:14.760 --> 01:13:18.600
It's appearing where it's
getting its semantic role

01:13:18.600 --> 01:13:21.660
as the object of like,
and it's appearing

01:13:21.660 --> 01:13:26.310
where it is being interpreted
as an operator arranging

01:13:26.310 --> 01:13:29.150
over the variable.

01:13:29.150 --> 01:13:33.060
And these examples proliferate
into pretty complicated cases

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:35.490
when you look at them closely.

01:13:35.490 --> 01:13:38.790
Turns out, interestingly,
that all of this

01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:45.180
follows if you develop an
optimal computational system,

01:13:45.180 --> 01:13:50.730
one that meets the condition
of perfect computational

01:13:50.730 --> 01:13:55.010
efficiency as the least possible
computational operations.

01:13:55.010 --> 01:13:59.030
Then it turns out that
what goes to the mind

01:13:59.030 --> 01:14:00.450
ought to have all these copies.

01:14:00.450 --> 01:14:02.340
They don't get erased.

01:14:02.340 --> 01:14:06.180
On the other hand, what goes to
the mouth has them all erased.

01:14:06.180 --> 01:14:08.460
Except for one to tell
you that it's there.

01:14:08.460 --> 01:14:11.230
That turns out to be
optimal computation.

01:14:11.230 --> 01:14:13.000
But that has a consequence.

01:14:13.000 --> 01:14:15.555
It means that
optimal computation,

01:14:15.555 --> 01:14:22.430
the core of language design, is
oriented towards the meaning.

01:14:22.430 --> 01:14:26.680
The mapping to the
transformation change

01:14:26.680 --> 01:14:29.370
to what comes out of
the mouth is causing

01:14:29.370 --> 01:14:31.470
communicative difficulties.

01:14:31.470 --> 01:14:34.560
Anyone who's ever worked
on a parsing program trying

01:14:34.560 --> 01:14:38.280
to figure out how to
mechanically interpret

01:14:38.280 --> 01:14:41.100
a sentence structure and
meaning of a sentence

01:14:41.100 --> 01:14:43.590
knows that one of
the hardest problems

01:14:43.590 --> 01:14:46.087
is what's called
filler gap problems.

01:14:46.087 --> 01:14:48.420
You hear the word what at the
beginning of the sentence,

01:14:48.420 --> 01:14:52.140
and you've got to
find where is the gap,

01:14:52.140 --> 01:14:55.680
the unpronounced element,
that this what is related to.

01:14:55.680 --> 01:14:58.780
If you pronounced them all,
that wouldn't be a problem.

01:14:58.780 --> 01:15:01.050
Most of the cases
would be solved.

01:15:01.050 --> 01:15:05.640
But efficient computation
leads to the consequence

01:15:05.640 --> 01:15:09.600
that it undermines
communicative efficiency.

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:19.710
That's pretty much like the case
of are eagles that flyings--

01:15:19.710 --> 01:15:21.840
are eagles that flying swim?

01:15:21.840 --> 01:15:24.390
Nice thought, but
you can't say it.

01:15:24.390 --> 01:15:29.460
And again, optimal
operation of the operations

01:15:29.460 --> 01:15:32.998
is leading to
communicative difficulties.

01:15:32.998 --> 01:15:35.040
And quite generally,
there's an interesting class

01:15:35.040 --> 01:15:37.890
of cases known now
where you have conflicts

01:15:37.890 --> 01:15:45.480
between computational efficiency
and communicative efficiency.

01:15:45.480 --> 01:15:48.420
And computational
efficiency wins hands down

01:15:48.420 --> 01:15:50.790
in every case that's
understood, which

01:15:50.790 --> 01:15:54.780
again suggests that
the design of language

01:15:54.780 --> 01:15:59.040
is really for meaning.

01:15:59.040 --> 01:16:03.370
It's for somatic and
intentional interpretation out

01:16:03.370 --> 01:16:05.760
of organized speech
acts and so on,

01:16:05.760 --> 01:16:08.660
and that the
externalization of it,

01:16:08.660 --> 01:16:14.880
the fact that it sort of appears
in the outside world somehow,

01:16:14.880 --> 01:16:17.190
is just a secondary process.

01:16:17.190 --> 01:16:19.800
Communication, tertiary process.

01:16:19.800 --> 01:16:22.380
Well, that reorients
our conception

01:16:22.380 --> 01:16:26.290
about how the mind works, but
it looks pretty plausible.

01:16:26.290 --> 01:16:28.935
Well another issue-- kind
of [? go ?] long sometimes.

01:16:28.935 --> 01:16:29.560
Another minute?

01:16:29.560 --> 01:16:31.220
Yeah.

01:16:31.220 --> 01:16:34.170
Going back to the '50s, it was
of course understood right away

01:16:34.170 --> 01:16:36.990
that none of this can--

01:16:36.990 --> 01:16:38.670
it's impossible for
any of these things

01:16:38.670 --> 01:16:43.660
to be learned by association,
conditioning, induction,

01:16:43.660 --> 01:16:46.110
any simple mechanism.

01:16:46.110 --> 01:16:49.800
So it must be the case that
these examples of the kind

01:16:49.800 --> 01:16:53.600
that I mentioned, which are
known very early by children,

01:16:53.600 --> 01:16:56.190
we have a little
evidence, they must

01:16:56.190 --> 01:16:59.640
result from the other
two possible factors

01:16:59.640 --> 01:17:01.210
in development.

01:17:01.210 --> 01:17:04.470
One of them is some
genetic endowment,

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:07.350
which is a species
property apparently.

01:17:07.350 --> 01:17:11.160
All humans share essentially
the same endowment.

01:17:11.160 --> 01:17:13.790
No group difference is known.

01:17:13.790 --> 01:17:14.550
So that's one.

01:17:14.550 --> 01:17:16.800
And the other is
just laws of nature.

01:17:16.800 --> 01:17:18.510
The kinds of laws
that determine,

01:17:18.510 --> 01:17:22.940
say, that a cell divides
into spheres, not tubes.

01:17:22.940 --> 01:17:27.400
So genetics is involved
some, but not much.

01:17:27.400 --> 01:17:31.620
The interaction of
these two factors

01:17:31.620 --> 01:17:35.440
ought to be able to yield
results of this kind.

01:17:35.440 --> 01:17:38.670
By now there's a fair amount
of evidence supporting

01:17:38.670 --> 01:17:41.760
that the kinds of things I
mentioned illustrate [? it. ?]

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:43.920
Of course, languages
differ all over the place.

01:17:43.920 --> 01:17:45.370
That was known too.

01:17:45.370 --> 01:17:50.340
So you have to show that
these principles that you're

01:17:50.340 --> 01:17:54.040
postulating are going
to apply universally.

01:17:54.040 --> 01:18:00.360
That does not mean, contrary to
a widely held misunderstanding

01:18:00.360 --> 01:18:02.220
that you can read in
journals like Nature

01:18:02.220 --> 01:18:04.380
and Science and
others, it does not

01:18:04.380 --> 01:18:08.230
mean that there are going to
be descriptive generalizations

01:18:08.230 --> 01:18:10.893
about language that
hold universally.

01:18:10.893 --> 01:18:12.310
In fact, it might
turn out there's

01:18:12.310 --> 01:18:14.230
no descriptive
generalizations at all,

01:18:14.230 --> 01:18:16.450
and you have a very
rich genetic structure.

01:18:16.450 --> 01:18:19.060
That's not the case,
but it could turn out,

01:18:19.060 --> 01:18:23.050
because the things that
you see are reflections

01:18:23.050 --> 01:18:25.540
of internal processes
that are going

01:18:25.540 --> 01:18:29.683
on that meet other conditions,
like efficient computation.

01:18:29.683 --> 01:18:31.100
But it must be
somehow that you've

01:18:31.100 --> 01:18:32.800
got to apply it to everything.

01:18:32.800 --> 01:18:36.500
Well, in the '50s,
in the golden age,

01:18:36.500 --> 01:18:38.200
a few languages were studied.

01:18:38.200 --> 01:18:42.700
The ones that people knew
in [INAUDIBLE] at that time.

01:18:42.700 --> 01:18:45.970
Actually, one of the
first extensive studies

01:18:45.970 --> 01:18:48.180
was on an American
Indian language.

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:51.787
That's the first dissertation
in the department.

01:18:51.787 --> 01:18:54.370
Actually, it was in the [? EE ?]
department, because we didn't

01:18:54.370 --> 01:18:56.230
have a formal department then.

01:18:56.230 --> 01:18:57.670
It was on Turkish.

01:18:57.670 --> 01:18:59.750
There was of course
work on English.

01:18:59.750 --> 01:19:01.960
[? Marcel ?] was
working on Russian.

01:19:01.960 --> 01:19:05.860
By the 1960s, as the
department developed

01:19:05.860 --> 01:19:09.130
and people came in from all
over, the range of languages

01:19:09.130 --> 01:19:11.770
investigated
expanded enormously.

01:19:11.770 --> 01:19:16.570
In the '80s and since,
it's just exploded.

01:19:16.570 --> 01:19:19.850
There is now intensive
investigation of languages,

01:19:19.850 --> 01:19:22.750
with a very wide type
typological variety.

01:19:22.750 --> 01:19:25.240
All kinds of new problems
being discovered,

01:19:25.240 --> 01:19:27.160
sometimes new answers.

01:19:27.160 --> 01:19:30.230
Sometimes they lead to
further problems and so on.

01:19:30.230 --> 01:19:33.910
At the same time, what
Steve mentioned before,

01:19:33.910 --> 01:19:36.670
the study of language
acquisition really took off.

01:19:36.670 --> 01:19:40.660
Actually, Steve's work
was part of the early work

01:19:40.660 --> 01:19:43.540
on this in the '80s.

01:19:43.540 --> 01:19:47.050
Based on trying to discover
how the option's [? a ?]

01:19:47.050 --> 01:19:49.660
variation of language,
which is all that

01:19:49.660 --> 01:19:51.130
has to be picked up by a child.

01:19:51.130 --> 01:19:52.690
The rest is fixed.

01:19:52.690 --> 01:19:55.070
How those are set on
the basis of data,

01:19:55.070 --> 01:19:58.600
and by now quite a
lot of work on that.

01:19:58.600 --> 01:20:02.520
All of this, I'll just finish by
saying that plenty of problems

01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:03.020
remain.

01:20:03.020 --> 01:20:05.080
I don't want to
overstate, but there's

01:20:05.080 --> 01:20:09.160
some fairly striking
and reasonably plausible

01:20:09.160 --> 01:20:12.730
conclusions which have
far-reaching implications.

01:20:12.730 --> 01:20:15.400
All of this, however,
is restricted

01:20:15.400 --> 01:20:21.310
to what's going on inside the
internal computing system.

01:20:21.310 --> 01:20:23.480
What's going on inside the mind.

01:20:23.480 --> 01:20:26.980
Now somehow, language has to be
related to the outside world.

01:20:26.980 --> 01:20:29.050
And because there
are two interfaces,

01:20:29.050 --> 01:20:32.110
it's going to have to be
related in two different ways.

01:20:32.110 --> 01:20:35.380
At the sensory motor
interface, the question

01:20:35.380 --> 01:20:38.740
is hard but sort of understood.

01:20:38.740 --> 01:20:43.310
That's the topic of the acoustic
and articulatory phonetics,

01:20:43.310 --> 01:20:46.110
which has been studied
pretty intensively.

01:20:46.110 --> 01:20:48.430
[? Orally, ?] too, for 60 years.

01:20:48.430 --> 01:20:49.750
And a lot of results.

01:20:49.750 --> 01:20:51.620
A lot of problems.

01:20:51.620 --> 01:20:54.440
But at least the
problem's kind of in hand.

01:20:54.440 --> 01:20:56.020
What about the other end?

01:20:56.020 --> 01:21:00.590
How does the meaning side
relate to the outside world?

01:21:00.590 --> 01:21:03.150
Well, there is a doctrine that's
widely held that's supposed

01:21:03.150 --> 01:21:04.780
to give an answer to this.

01:21:04.780 --> 01:21:08.310
It's sometimes called the
referentialist doctrine.

01:21:08.310 --> 01:21:12.640
It holds that the elementary
semantic units, let's

01:21:12.640 --> 01:21:18.390
say words for simplicity, they
relate to the outside world

01:21:18.390 --> 01:21:22.870
by a relation of reference,
picking something out,

01:21:22.870 --> 01:21:27.040
or denotation for predicates,
picking out a class of things.

01:21:27.040 --> 01:21:29.890
So like cow, the
word cow picks out

01:21:29.890 --> 01:21:33.640
cows where cows are something
that a physicist could identify

01:21:33.640 --> 01:21:35.110
in principle.

01:21:35.110 --> 01:21:37.650
The only problem-- and
that's very widely held.

01:21:37.650 --> 01:21:41.845
It was known in classical
Greece that that's not correct.

01:21:41.845 --> 01:21:43.720
They didn't have much
of a deep analysis,

01:21:43.720 --> 01:21:46.450
but examples showing
it doesn't work.

01:21:46.450 --> 01:21:49.790
In the 17th century,
in what we ought

01:21:49.790 --> 01:21:52.670
to call the first
cognitive revolution,

01:21:52.670 --> 01:21:54.340
many of the
significant questions

01:21:54.340 --> 01:21:58.810
were raised, but are
still puzzling today.

01:21:58.810 --> 01:22:03.670
It was widely understood
that that doesn't work.

01:22:03.670 --> 01:22:06.010
As Hume, David Hume
put it, summarizing

01:22:06.010 --> 01:22:13.300
a century of inquiry, that
the way we categorize things--

01:22:13.300 --> 01:22:14.810
the things we
categorize, he said,

01:22:14.810 --> 01:22:20.740
are fictitious, created by the
mind using Gestalt properties,

01:22:20.740 --> 01:22:25.540
notions of cause and effect,
notions of psychic continuity

01:22:25.540 --> 01:22:27.520
for sentient objects.

01:22:27.520 --> 01:22:29.320
John Locke studied this.

01:22:29.320 --> 01:22:34.850
And other properties that a
physicist cannot identify.

01:22:34.850 --> 01:22:39.140
They are imposed by the mind
on reflexive experience.

01:22:39.140 --> 01:22:42.910
And those are the
entities, if you like, sort

01:22:42.910 --> 01:22:45.590
of mental entities
that we construct.

01:22:45.590 --> 01:22:47.425
And they're somehow
related to the world.

01:22:47.425 --> 01:22:50.210
Now, this poses a huge problem.

01:22:50.210 --> 01:22:52.720
There's no animal
communication system known

01:22:52.720 --> 01:22:55.320
that has anything
remotely like this.

01:22:55.320 --> 01:22:59.300
There, the referentialist
doctrine apparently holds.

01:22:59.300 --> 01:23:04.870
So if you take say, cries
of a vervet monkey, each cry

01:23:04.870 --> 01:23:09.580
is key to some identifiable
physical event.

01:23:09.580 --> 01:23:11.950
Like leaves are
moving, we interpret

01:23:11.950 --> 01:23:14.950
that as meaning an eagle's
coming, all the vervets run

01:23:14.950 --> 01:23:15.758
and hide.

01:23:15.758 --> 01:23:16.300
I don't know.

01:23:16.300 --> 01:23:18.010
How they interpret
is another question.

01:23:18.010 --> 01:23:23.350
But or else it's an internal
sort of hormonal phenomena,

01:23:23.350 --> 01:23:25.870
like I'm hungry or
something like that.

01:23:25.870 --> 01:23:28.990
Every communication system
that's known works like that,

01:23:28.990 --> 01:23:33.743
but human language doesn't
work like that at all.

01:23:33.743 --> 01:23:36.160
You take a look at the words
of the language, none of them

01:23:36.160 --> 01:23:37.680
work like that.

01:23:37.680 --> 01:23:39.310
And that's a real problem.

01:23:39.310 --> 01:23:41.560
For one thing, it's a
tremendous evolutionary problem.

01:23:41.560 --> 01:23:44.020
Like where did all
this come from?

01:23:44.020 --> 01:23:47.320
Totally unknown, and
maybe unknowable,

01:23:47.320 --> 01:23:51.160
but it's also a problem
for the nature of language

01:23:51.160 --> 01:23:53.080
and for the acquisition
of language.

01:23:53.080 --> 01:23:56.740
Like how do children find
these properties which they

01:23:56.740 --> 01:23:58.840
get to understand very quickly?

01:23:58.840 --> 01:24:02.200
In fact, things like
children's fairy tales

01:24:02.200 --> 01:24:05.020
exploit them effectively.

01:24:05.020 --> 01:24:08.440
Well, these are
major mysteries that

01:24:08.440 --> 01:24:12.310
remain on the horizon
right at the core of what

01:24:12.310 --> 01:24:14.140
language and thought are about.

01:24:14.140 --> 01:24:15.630
And there are plenty
of other ones.

01:24:15.630 --> 01:24:19.540
So I'd just suggest again that
the capacity to be puzzled

01:24:19.540 --> 01:24:21.690
is a good one to cultivate.

01:24:33.450 --> 01:24:34.950
PINKER: Thank you
very much, Noam.

01:24:34.950 --> 01:24:35.760
Emilio Bizzi.

01:24:45.840 --> 01:24:48.360
BIZZI: Well, one of the--

01:24:48.360 --> 01:24:49.470
can you hear me?

01:24:49.470 --> 01:24:50.550
Yeah?

01:24:50.550 --> 01:24:54.000
One of the goals of
artificial intelligence,

01:24:54.000 --> 01:24:59.640
as I understand it, was to
build intelligent machines.

01:24:59.640 --> 01:25:02.880
And there's a class, of course,
of intelligence machines

01:25:02.880 --> 01:25:06.190
that move like robots.

01:25:06.190 --> 01:25:16.740
And so what I'm going
to discuss is movement,

01:25:16.740 --> 01:25:21.960
not in machines, but movement
from a biological perspective.

01:25:21.960 --> 01:25:26.220
And then toward the end, I
will discuss the interaction

01:25:26.220 --> 01:25:30.330
between artificial systems like
robots and biological systems

01:25:30.330 --> 01:25:31.210
that move.

01:25:31.210 --> 01:25:36.150
But for the initial
part of my discussion

01:25:36.150 --> 01:25:40.470
here, I like to
point out something

01:25:40.470 --> 01:25:42.570
that I can illustrate
with an example.

01:25:45.330 --> 01:25:50.750
Let's say that I want to
reach this pair of glasses.

01:25:50.750 --> 01:25:54.360
Well, this is something
extremely simple.

01:25:54.360 --> 01:25:58.650
We do it hundreds
of times per day.

01:25:58.650 --> 01:26:02.640
We have done it for thousands
and thousands of times.

01:26:02.640 --> 01:26:05.000
So what's the big deal about it?

01:26:05.000 --> 01:26:12.230
Well, this simple gesture here
points out an important fact.

01:26:12.230 --> 01:26:16.940
And that is that what seems
to be a very simple action,

01:26:16.940 --> 01:26:22.460
in reality, the neural
processes that subserve,

01:26:22.460 --> 01:26:27.620
that make it possible for
this action to be expressed,

01:26:27.620 --> 01:26:30.780
are of great complexity.

01:26:30.780 --> 01:26:35.540
And this complexity is, to a
certain extent, mysterious.

01:26:35.540 --> 01:26:38.840
We know a little bit about it.

01:26:38.840 --> 01:26:41.840
We know that some
of the problem, some

01:26:41.840 --> 01:26:44.750
of the computations, are
beginning to be seen.

01:26:44.750 --> 01:26:50.270
But there are an
enormous amount of things

01:26:50.270 --> 01:26:55.520
that have to do with
actions that at this point

01:26:55.520 --> 01:26:57.650
remain fairly mysterious.

01:26:57.650 --> 01:27:03.830
So let me start with what
we have glimpsed recently

01:27:03.830 --> 01:27:05.700
in the last few years.

01:27:05.700 --> 01:27:10.410
And that has to do with-- and
I'll go back to this example.

01:27:10.410 --> 01:27:15.410
If I want to touch
this pair of glasses,

01:27:15.410 --> 01:27:19.250
then I have to do a number
of things simultaneously.

01:27:19.250 --> 01:27:22.940
I have to move my eyes,
my eye muscles, my neck

01:27:22.940 --> 01:27:26.360
muscles, my trunk muscles,
and of course, then

01:27:26.360 --> 01:27:27.800
there arm muscles.

01:27:27.800 --> 01:27:31.920
Now, from an anatomical
point of view,

01:27:31.920 --> 01:27:34.670
all these muscles
are made of elements,

01:27:34.670 --> 01:27:36.850
which are the muscle fibers.

01:27:36.850 --> 01:27:43.750
And each group of muscle
fibers receives a fiber

01:27:43.750 --> 01:27:46.180
from the central nervous
system, and particularly from,

01:27:46.180 --> 01:27:48.680
in this case, from
the spinal cord.

01:27:48.680 --> 01:27:53.770
So what we have here is that to
implement this simple gesture,

01:27:53.770 --> 01:27:56.340
the central nervous
system has to control

01:27:56.340 --> 01:28:03.250
an enormous [? piece, ?] an
enormous number of elements.

01:28:03.250 --> 01:28:07.420
That is the muscle fibers
that make up the muscles.

01:28:11.140 --> 01:28:15.610
So this is in a sense
a gigantic problem,

01:28:15.610 --> 01:28:20.080
because we have an extremely
tough computational problem.

01:28:20.080 --> 01:28:25.990
How to arrange this
distribution of signals

01:28:25.990 --> 01:28:28.200
to a very vast space.

01:28:28.200 --> 01:28:35.380
Well, in the last few years,
a number of investigators

01:28:35.380 --> 01:28:42.660
have identified elements
of the architecture

01:28:42.660 --> 01:28:46.350
of the central nervous system
that deals with movement that

01:28:46.350 --> 01:28:50.040
has indicated a modularity.

01:28:50.040 --> 01:28:54.510
And this modularity has been
identified predominantly

01:28:54.510 --> 01:28:55.630
in the spinal cord.

01:28:55.630 --> 01:29:00.460
It's maybe also the upper
part of the brain stem.

01:29:00.460 --> 01:29:03.760
But in any case, most of
it is in the spinal cord.

01:29:03.760 --> 01:29:06.030
And what it does,
it means that there

01:29:06.030 --> 01:29:13.880
are groups of interneurons
that manage to put together

01:29:13.880 --> 01:29:16.950
in a unit a group of muscles.

01:29:16.950 --> 01:29:17.920
What does that mean?

01:29:17.920 --> 01:29:20.040
It means that the
number of degrees

01:29:20.040 --> 01:29:25.350
of freedom, this vast space,
has been reduced dramatically

01:29:25.350 --> 01:29:28.080
in such a way that
the descending

01:29:28.080 --> 01:29:33.150
fibers from the cortex
that convey from the brain

01:29:33.150 --> 01:29:38.790
down to the spinal cord the
information for movement, all

01:29:38.790 --> 01:29:42.900
they have to do is to combine.

01:29:42.900 --> 01:29:48.420
They activate these various
modules in the spinal cord,

01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:50.910
combine them, and
provide coefficient

01:29:50.910 --> 01:29:53.070
of activations for each one.

01:29:53.070 --> 01:30:00.570
And this view of how
the brain manages

01:30:00.570 --> 01:30:04.860
to be so effective
in producing movement

01:30:04.860 --> 01:30:09.470
is derived from experimental
work that in this laboratory,

01:30:09.470 --> 01:30:11.820
in my laboratory, and
in other laboratories,

01:30:11.820 --> 01:30:16.270
has been performed
in the last 10 years.

01:30:16.270 --> 01:30:19.710
Now there are other things
that are more mysterious.

01:30:19.710 --> 01:30:26.190
When I do this simple movement
here, this movement is learned,

01:30:26.190 --> 01:30:31.500
has been learned somehow
during the course of life.

01:30:31.500 --> 01:30:36.210
The site of learning,
motor learning,

01:30:36.210 --> 01:30:41.650
is certainly the motor
cortex of the brain.

01:30:41.650 --> 01:30:44.070
So the frontal areas
of the frontal lobe.

01:30:44.070 --> 01:30:46.750
Now, these areas of the
frontal lobe of course

01:30:46.750 --> 01:30:51.940
connect it with the subcortical
nuclei, with the cerebellum,

01:30:51.940 --> 01:30:52.710
and so on.

01:30:52.710 --> 01:30:57.330
And certainly all these
areas, in conjunction

01:30:57.330 --> 01:31:00.030
with the connectivity
of the spinal cord,

01:31:00.030 --> 01:31:05.820
represent the circuitry that
encode the signals, the memory

01:31:05.820 --> 01:31:10.230
signals that are necessary
to do this simple action.

01:31:10.230 --> 01:31:12.165
But here there is a catch.

01:31:14.850 --> 01:31:17.640
When I do these
simple actions, I

01:31:17.640 --> 01:31:20.430
can do them like in
front of you now,

01:31:20.430 --> 01:31:25.110
but I can do it by moving my
body in a different posture.

01:31:25.110 --> 01:31:27.600
I can be reclining,
and nevertheless I

01:31:27.600 --> 01:31:30.370
can accomplish the same goal.

01:31:30.370 --> 01:31:37.500
So this is-- it's very tough to
understand how the signals that

01:31:37.500 --> 01:31:42.270
have been memorized, which
specify particular muscles that

01:31:42.270 --> 01:31:45.300
have to be activated in
that particular context

01:31:45.300 --> 01:31:49.120
can, in another context,
still be just as effective.

01:31:49.120 --> 01:31:51.540
So this is a question
of generalization.

01:31:51.540 --> 01:31:57.030
And we-- at least
I don't understand

01:31:57.030 --> 01:32:00.090
how the central
nervous system manages

01:32:00.090 --> 01:32:06.320
to do this process of
generalization so effectively.

01:32:06.320 --> 01:32:12.190
There are also other
computational problems

01:32:12.190 --> 01:32:19.120
that are quite difficult to
see how they are implemented

01:32:19.120 --> 01:32:20.800
in the central nervous system.

01:32:20.800 --> 01:32:24.640
And that is I can reach
this pair of glasses.

01:32:24.640 --> 01:32:27.220
If there is an obstacle,
I can go up this way,

01:32:27.220 --> 01:32:28.730
I can go down this way.

01:32:28.730 --> 01:32:31.390
So this is a
question of planning.

01:32:31.390 --> 01:32:33.880
How [? batteries ?]
are extremely

01:32:33.880 --> 01:32:40.900
good at planning their action
depending on the environment.

01:32:40.900 --> 01:32:49.690
And how, again, this memory
that guides the movements,

01:32:49.690 --> 01:32:55.300
how can that be translated into
[? when ?] a signal is modified

01:32:55.300 --> 01:33:00.420
in order to accomplish
pathways that each time

01:33:00.420 --> 01:33:03.090
are potentially different.

01:33:03.090 --> 01:33:05.500
It depends on the environment.

01:33:05.500 --> 01:33:12.700
Now there are other things that
are somewhat less mysterious.

01:33:12.700 --> 01:33:18.970
We know that among
the vertebrates,

01:33:18.970 --> 01:33:24.230
a certain amount of learning
goes through by imitation.

01:33:24.230 --> 01:33:27.490
And recently, some
light has been

01:33:27.490 --> 01:33:32.020
shed on this process of
learning by imitation.

01:33:32.020 --> 01:33:35.950
Neurons have been found
in the frontal lobe that

01:33:35.950 --> 01:33:40.760
discharge when the
subject sees an action.

01:33:40.760 --> 01:33:44.470
And when the subject
repeats that action,

01:33:44.470 --> 01:33:45.460
the similar action.

01:33:45.460 --> 01:33:47.680
So these are called
mirror neurons,

01:33:47.680 --> 01:33:52.570
and they are an important
feature of the model system

01:33:52.570 --> 01:34:03.360
that seem to provide the basis
for learning by imitation.

01:34:03.360 --> 01:34:09.550
Now although there are all these
tough computational problems

01:34:09.550 --> 01:34:13.000
that need to be
understood, I am optimistic

01:34:13.000 --> 01:34:19.060
that in the next few years we
will make a lot of progress.

01:34:19.060 --> 01:34:24.220
And the reason is that there
are many laboratories scattered

01:34:24.220 --> 01:34:27.760
in various parts
of the world that

01:34:27.760 --> 01:34:34.930
are pursuing the issues of
robotics, humanoid robotics.

01:34:34.930 --> 01:34:37.120
That is, what they
are trying to do

01:34:37.120 --> 01:34:41.230
is to put these properties
that I've described to you,

01:34:41.230 --> 01:34:46.920
generalization, planning,
learning by imitation,

01:34:46.920 --> 01:34:49.450
and so on in machines.

01:34:49.450 --> 01:34:54.070
And I don't know how
far this effort--

01:34:54.070 --> 01:34:56.980
how much these people
have accomplished.

01:34:56.980 --> 01:34:59.530
But nevertheless,
the fact that there

01:34:59.530 --> 01:35:06.910
is this intellectual attitude
toward implementing in machines

01:35:06.910 --> 01:35:09.430
[? lay ?] solutions
to try to find

01:35:09.430 --> 01:35:12.100
the computational
solutions that provide

01:35:12.100 --> 01:35:20.010
these properties to machines
is a way to really understand,

01:35:20.010 --> 01:35:21.820
is a way to understand.

01:35:21.820 --> 01:35:25.300
When you start to
make things, beginning

01:35:25.300 --> 01:35:30.910
to face the computational
problems, and understand.

01:35:30.910 --> 01:35:34.360
There is also-- and I'm
finishing [INAUDIBLE]..

01:35:34.360 --> 01:35:44.760
There is also a new field,
which is somewhat promising.

01:35:44.760 --> 01:35:49.850
And that is to place
sets of microelectrodes

01:35:49.850 --> 01:35:53.520
into the brain of--

01:35:53.520 --> 01:35:56.250
it has been tried with
humans, and is usually

01:35:56.250 --> 01:36:00.600
tried in animals, and
connect the output

01:36:00.600 --> 01:36:05.190
of the signals that come
out of these microelectrodes

01:36:05.190 --> 01:36:06.650
and connect it with machines.

01:36:06.650 --> 01:36:12.220
So this is a brain-machine
interaction, which obviously

01:36:12.220 --> 01:36:18.100
has tremendous importance,
both in understanding

01:36:18.100 --> 01:36:20.530
the workings of the
central nervous system,

01:36:20.530 --> 01:36:27.280
and also the the practical
significance in rehabilitation

01:36:27.280 --> 01:36:29.560
of amputees and so on.

01:36:29.560 --> 01:36:34.660
Now for the time being,
there are technical problems

01:36:34.660 --> 01:36:39.280
that prevent the
quick implementation

01:36:39.280 --> 01:36:41.740
of brain-machine interactions.

01:36:41.740 --> 01:36:44.800
And those that have
to do with the brain

01:36:44.800 --> 01:36:50.500
reacts to the contact with
these permanent [? contact ?]

01:36:50.500 --> 01:36:53.550
with these electrodes
in the brain.

01:36:53.550 --> 01:36:55.870
They are essentially
rejected after a while.

01:36:55.870 --> 01:37:03.250
But it's conceivable
that more different types

01:37:03.250 --> 01:37:09.760
of different technologies could
be used in order to get around

01:37:09.760 --> 01:37:10.640
this problem.

01:37:10.640 --> 01:37:14.410
So this is the two reasons
why I have a certain amount

01:37:14.410 --> 01:37:21.680
of optimism that for the
future, this [? methodic ?]

01:37:21.680 --> 01:37:23.830
intelligence will be understood.

01:37:23.830 --> 01:37:24.330
Thank you.

01:37:33.910 --> 01:37:35.110
PINKER: Thank, you Emilio.

01:37:35.110 --> 01:37:35.890
Barbara Partee.

01:37:46.070 --> 01:37:49.580
PARTEE: For me, the adventure
began just 50 years ago, here

01:37:49.580 --> 01:37:52.510
at MIT in 1961.

01:37:52.510 --> 01:37:56.120
The Chomskyan revolution
had just begun,

01:37:56.120 --> 01:37:59.660
and Noam Chomsky and Morris
Halle had just opened up

01:37:59.660 --> 01:38:01.980
a PhD program in linguistics.

01:38:01.980 --> 01:38:04.550
And I came in the first class.

01:38:04.550 --> 01:38:07.340
I want to start by
thanking Chomsky and Halle

01:38:07.340 --> 01:38:09.260
for building that program.

01:38:09.260 --> 01:38:12.770
And I thank MIT and the Research
Laboratory of Electronics

01:38:12.770 --> 01:38:14.420
for supporting it.

01:38:14.420 --> 01:38:17.000
I'm indebted to Chomsky
for revolutionizing

01:38:17.000 --> 01:38:20.420
the field of linguistics and
making it into a field whose

01:38:20.420 --> 01:38:22.790
excitement has never waned.

01:38:22.790 --> 01:38:25.190
Chomsky redefined
linguistics as the study

01:38:25.190 --> 01:38:27.830
of human linguistic
competence, making

01:38:27.830 --> 01:38:33.080
linguistics one of the early
pillars of cognitive science.

01:38:33.080 --> 01:38:35.210
At the center of the
Chomskyan revolution

01:38:35.210 --> 01:38:39.590
was syntax, generative
grammar, a finite specification

01:38:39.590 --> 01:38:43.100
of the infinite set of
sentences of a language.

01:38:43.100 --> 01:38:46.860
That launched an extremely
productive research program,

01:38:46.860 --> 01:38:49.280
but it didn't include semantics.

01:38:49.280 --> 01:38:52.970
Chomsky considered meaning
scientifically intractable,

01:38:52.970 --> 01:38:55.250
and he wasn't alone.

01:38:55.250 --> 01:38:59.390
When linguists did soon tried
to add semantics generative

01:38:59.390 --> 01:39:02.570
grammar, there were
two big obstacles.

01:39:02.570 --> 01:39:06.020
A lack of good formal
tools, and the absence

01:39:06.020 --> 01:39:09.980
of any non-subjective
notion of meaning.

01:39:09.980 --> 01:39:12.710
It was the UCLA logician
Richard Montague

01:39:12.710 --> 01:39:18.120
who revolutionized the study
of semantics in the late 1960s.

01:39:18.120 --> 01:39:20.720
He built on successes
in formalizing

01:39:20.720 --> 01:39:24.170
the syntax and semantics
of logical languages,

01:39:24.170 --> 01:39:27.520
the work of Frege,
Tarski, and others.

01:39:27.520 --> 01:39:30.410
Montague himself
developed a higher order

01:39:30.410 --> 01:39:34.250
typed intentional logic
with a model theory,

01:39:34.250 --> 01:39:36.890
and with its help, he
was able to formalize

01:39:36.890 --> 01:39:40.730
a significant part of the
syntax and semantics of English

01:39:40.730 --> 01:39:44.450
within a clearly articulated
theoretical framework.

01:39:44.450 --> 01:39:48.190
The result was what we
now call formal semantics.

01:39:48.190 --> 01:39:51.380
Emmon Bach has summed up
Chomsky's and Montague's

01:39:51.380 --> 01:39:53.990
cumulative innovations thus.

01:39:53.990 --> 01:39:56.030
Chomsky showed
that English can be

01:39:56.030 --> 01:39:58.310
described as a formal system.

01:39:58.310 --> 01:39:59.900
Montague showed
that English can be

01:39:59.900 --> 01:40:03.890
described as an
interpreted formal system.

01:40:03.890 --> 01:40:08.510
For me, as a young syntactician
at UCLA in the late '60s,

01:40:08.510 --> 01:40:11.360
Montague's work was eye-opening.

01:40:11.360 --> 01:40:13.940
Linguists had been
building tree-like semantic

01:40:13.940 --> 01:40:15.650
representations.

01:40:15.650 --> 01:40:19.490
Okay for capturing certain
things like scope ambiguity,

01:40:19.490 --> 01:40:22.790
but otherwise leading
to endless arguments

01:40:22.790 --> 01:40:26.270
about competing representations.

01:40:26.270 --> 01:40:29.090
We linguists never dreamed
of truth conditions

01:40:29.090 --> 01:40:31.470
being relevant for linguistics.

01:40:31.470 --> 01:40:34.730
But Montague's work, and
David Lewis's, showed

01:40:34.730 --> 01:40:38.570
that truth conditions are
crucial for giving semantics

01:40:38.570 --> 01:40:41.220
a non-subjective foundation.

01:40:41.220 --> 01:40:43.610
A linguistically
exciting part was

01:40:43.610 --> 01:40:45.800
that with such a
rich logic, we could

01:40:45.800 --> 01:40:49.520
get a close match between
syntactic constituents

01:40:49.520 --> 01:40:51.560
and semantic constituents.

01:40:51.560 --> 01:40:54.710
And meanings of sentences
could be built up recursively

01:40:54.710 --> 01:40:58.900
and compositionally from
the meanings of their parts,

01:40:58.900 --> 01:41:01.100
of their syntactic parts.

01:41:01.100 --> 01:41:03.020
One brief illustration.

01:41:03.020 --> 01:41:06.800
Bertrand Russell had argued
that natural language syntax

01:41:06.800 --> 01:41:09.590
is logically very
misleading, since it

01:41:09.590 --> 01:41:14.660
puts John and every man into the
same syntactic category of noun

01:41:14.660 --> 01:41:16.040
phrases.

01:41:16.040 --> 01:41:19.250
Whereas in first order
logic, John walks,

01:41:19.250 --> 01:41:22.580
and every man walks, must
have radically different

01:41:22.580 --> 01:41:24.920
syntactic structures.

01:41:24.920 --> 01:41:27.920
Montague showed that natural
language syntax does not

01:41:27.920 --> 01:41:30.030
have to be logically
misleading at all.

01:41:30.030 --> 01:41:35.000
All noun phrases can be treated
uniformly in higher order logic

01:41:35.000 --> 01:41:37.730
as so-called
generalized quantifiers.

01:41:37.730 --> 01:41:40.580
And English
subject-predicate sentences,

01:41:40.580 --> 01:41:43.370
like John walks,
and every man walks,

01:41:43.370 --> 01:41:46.010
can then be interpreted
in a similar manner

01:41:46.010 --> 01:41:47.710
straightforwardly.

01:41:47.710 --> 01:41:50.120
Generalized quantifier
theory is now

01:41:50.120 --> 01:41:53.660
one of many rich topics
in formal semantics,

01:41:53.660 --> 01:41:55.880
and it can explain many
things that we couldn't

01:41:55.880 --> 01:41:59.100
explain with just syntax.

01:41:59.100 --> 01:42:02.970
I started working on Montague
grammar in about 1970

01:42:02.970 --> 01:42:06.090
to try to integrate it into
linguistics by finding ways

01:42:06.090 --> 01:42:09.240
to put Chomsky's and
Montague's work together.

01:42:09.240 --> 01:42:12.030
From the early '70s,
collaborative work

01:42:12.030 --> 01:42:14.940
among philosophers,
logicians, and linguists

01:42:14.940 --> 01:42:19.320
in the US and Europe
built in many directions.

01:42:19.320 --> 01:42:22.830
One important result is that
with a serious semantics

01:42:22.830 --> 01:42:25.200
to share the load,
syntax doesn't

01:42:25.200 --> 01:42:29.130
have to do all of the work
that we once imagined.

01:42:29.130 --> 01:42:33.820
Syntactic and semantic advances
now often develop in tandem,

01:42:33.820 --> 01:42:36.890
each informing the other.

01:42:36.890 --> 01:42:39.860
By the late 1980s,
formal semantics

01:42:39.860 --> 01:42:42.410
was well established within
linguistics departments

01:42:42.410 --> 01:42:43.660
in the US.

01:42:43.660 --> 01:42:48.270
In Europe, it may be in
linguistics or in philosophy.

01:42:48.270 --> 01:42:51.600
Among middle generation
leaders of formal semantics,

01:42:51.600 --> 01:42:55.470
I'll just mention two who are
involved in this symposium

01:42:55.470 --> 01:42:59.520
and who were my PhD students
at UMass in the 1980s.

01:42:59.520 --> 01:43:02.280
Irene Heim, head of the
Department of Linguistics

01:43:02.280 --> 01:43:05.070
and Philosophy
here, became MIT's

01:43:05.070 --> 01:43:08.970
first formal semantics
faculty member in 1989.

01:43:08.970 --> 01:43:12.930
And Gennaro Chierchia is now
Head of Linguistics at Harvard.

01:43:12.930 --> 01:43:15.150
Both have made seminal
contributions that

01:43:15.150 --> 01:43:18.210
have helped shape the field.

01:43:18.210 --> 01:43:21.480
I have to mention one
seeming paradox which

01:43:21.480 --> 01:43:27.180
relates to open challenges
as well as to past progress.

01:43:27.180 --> 01:43:30.390
I stressed how the Chomskyan
revolution made linguistics

01:43:30.390 --> 01:43:33.390
an early pillar of
cognitive science.

01:43:33.390 --> 01:43:38.970
Yet Frege's anti-psychologistic
stance, shared by Montague,

01:43:38.970 --> 01:43:42.990
was crucial in the foundations
of formal semantics.

01:43:42.990 --> 01:43:46.050
Frege argued that truth
conditions, and not

01:43:46.050 --> 01:43:49.140
mental ideas, have
to be at the core

01:43:49.140 --> 01:43:51.600
of the meaning of a sentence.

01:43:51.600 --> 01:43:54.870
And the work of generations
of linguists and philosophers

01:43:54.870 --> 01:43:57.930
has shown the fruitfulness
of that approach.

01:43:57.930 --> 01:44:01.200
First, we have to formalize what
the semantics of our language

01:44:01.200 --> 01:44:06.600
is, what our sentences say about
how the world is, then figure

01:44:06.600 --> 01:44:10.240
out how our knowledge
of it, such as it is,

01:44:10.240 --> 01:44:13.170
is acquired and exploited.

01:44:13.170 --> 01:44:16.380
This stance might seem to
exclude formal semantics

01:44:16.380 --> 01:44:20.020
from cognitive science, but
I believe on the contrary,

01:44:20.020 --> 01:44:21.960
it makes the
contributions of semantics

01:44:21.960 --> 01:44:24.930
to cognitive science all
the more interesting.

01:44:24.930 --> 01:44:27.960
Human language is a
remarkable achievement.

01:44:27.960 --> 01:44:30.990
Part of what's remarkable
is how we implicitly

01:44:30.990 --> 01:44:35.980
recognize and navigate the
social construction of meaning.

01:44:35.980 --> 01:44:38.340
When we converse,
we simultaneously

01:44:38.340 --> 01:44:41.850
exchange information
and negotiate meaning.

01:44:41.850 --> 01:44:45.270
David Lewis's classic
work on convention

01:44:45.270 --> 01:44:47.460
was an early
milestone in exploring

01:44:47.460 --> 01:44:50.550
the relation between
individual competence, what's

01:44:50.550 --> 01:44:52.710
in the head of
one language user,

01:44:52.710 --> 01:44:55.980
and social intelligence, an
important dimension that is

01:44:55.980 --> 01:45:00.320
still probably under-explored.

01:45:00.320 --> 01:45:03.020
As for newer
directions, the best

01:45:03.020 --> 01:45:05.750
understood parts of
formal semantics concern

01:45:05.750 --> 01:45:10.350
the semantic compositions
of parts from wholes.

01:45:10.350 --> 01:45:11.450
No, of wholes-- sorry.

01:45:11.450 --> 01:45:13.440
Of wholes from parts, of course.

01:45:13.440 --> 01:45:15.020
Yeah.

01:45:15.020 --> 01:45:19.790
The semantics of-- I
proofread this several times.

01:45:19.790 --> 01:45:23.030
What we might call the
semantics of syntax.

01:45:23.030 --> 01:45:24.830
There has also
been great progress

01:45:24.830 --> 01:45:28.280
on formalizing parts
of pragmatics involving

01:45:28.280 --> 01:45:32.340
the interaction of meaning,
language use, and context,

01:45:32.340 --> 01:45:35.870
including the study of how
context both affects and is

01:45:35.870 --> 01:45:38.750
affected by interpretation.

01:45:38.750 --> 01:45:40.730
And studies in
language processing

01:45:40.730 --> 01:45:43.580
now include formal
semantics and pragmatics.

01:45:43.580 --> 01:45:47.690
And game theoretic approaches
are having a growing influence.

01:45:47.690 --> 01:45:50.150
Computational formal
semantics is now

01:45:50.150 --> 01:45:52.880
a subfield of
computational linguistics,

01:45:52.880 --> 01:45:57.050
contributing to both
theoretical and applied goals.

01:45:57.050 --> 01:46:00.680
And there's promising early
work on universals and typology

01:46:00.680 --> 01:46:03.770
in semantics with
innovative fieldwork

01:46:03.770 --> 01:46:06.620
uncovering semantic and
pragmatic subtleties

01:46:06.620 --> 01:46:09.120
of various indigenous languages.

01:46:09.120 --> 01:46:11.810
And just as in
syntax, such languages

01:46:11.810 --> 01:46:16.190
prove to be as semantically
rich and complex as more

01:46:16.190 --> 01:46:20.240
familiar European languages.

01:46:20.240 --> 01:46:23.420
There are many challenges
facing formal semantics

01:46:23.420 --> 01:46:24.990
and its relatives.

01:46:24.990 --> 01:46:27.500
These are still young fields.

01:46:27.500 --> 01:46:29.870
I'll mention just
two that I think

01:46:29.870 --> 01:46:32.300
are important,
both for the field

01:46:32.300 --> 01:46:35.150
and for the goals
of this symposium.

01:46:35.150 --> 01:46:38.300
First, the semantics of
[? sentencial ?] structures

01:46:38.300 --> 01:46:42.200
is increasingly well-understood,
but lexical semantics

01:46:42.200 --> 01:46:46.310
is really hard, and formal
methods are weakest here.

01:46:46.310 --> 01:46:50.930
It's in lexical semantics that
we most clearly cannot equate

01:46:50.930 --> 01:46:53.840
the language with
our knowledge of it.

01:46:53.840 --> 01:46:56.820
As Hilary Putnam
observed years ago,

01:46:56.820 --> 01:46:59.780
the fact that he doesn't
himself know anything

01:46:59.780 --> 01:47:03.320
that would distinguish a
beech tree from an elm tree

01:47:03.320 --> 01:47:09.140
does not mean that beech and elm
are synonyms in his language.

01:47:09.140 --> 01:47:11.840
The lexicon has lots of
linguistically important

01:47:11.840 --> 01:47:15.170
substructure, but it's
also the part of language

01:47:15.170 --> 01:47:19.340
that interfaces most strongly
with non-linguistic knowledge,

01:47:19.340 --> 01:47:23.360
with encyclopedic knowledge,
with our commonsense ontology,

01:47:23.360 --> 01:47:26.780
with visual imagery, with
cultural associations,

01:47:26.780 --> 01:47:29.170
and on and on.

01:47:29.170 --> 01:47:31.930
And connected with
that, another challenge

01:47:31.930 --> 01:47:35.920
is how to build formal semantics
into real-time processing

01:47:35.920 --> 01:47:39.340
models, whether we think of
psychological models of how

01:47:39.340 --> 01:47:42.100
we do it or
computational models that

01:47:42.100 --> 01:47:44.740
might do it in an applied way.

01:47:44.740 --> 01:47:47.050
Models that involve
the integration

01:47:47.050 --> 01:47:50.290
of linguistic, and not
specifically linguistic

01:47:50.290 --> 01:47:52.080
knowledge.

01:47:52.080 --> 01:47:56.370
This concerns not only lexical
semantics, but also all kinds

01:47:56.370 --> 01:47:58.200
of context dependence.

01:47:58.200 --> 01:48:01.380
Implicit content,
meaning shifts,

01:48:01.380 --> 01:48:04.260
and nonliteral uses of language.

01:48:04.260 --> 01:48:05.860
I know there is progress.

01:48:05.860 --> 01:48:08.110
I'm not an expert
in those areas.

01:48:08.110 --> 01:48:11.400
But I believe this is a domain
where major interdisciplinary

01:48:11.400 --> 01:48:15.030
innovation and cooperation
may be crucial.

01:48:15.030 --> 01:48:18.000
Just an illustration
of the challenges.

01:48:18.000 --> 01:48:22.350
I'm pretty sure that the most
successful search engines

01:48:22.350 --> 01:48:26.940
do not do any formal semantic
analysis of questions

01:48:26.940 --> 01:48:30.780
that we ask or of texts
that they are searching.

01:48:30.780 --> 01:48:33.750
I've heard of some
beginnings of some nice work

01:48:33.750 --> 01:48:37.890
in that direction, but large
scale, all-purpose, fast search

01:48:37.890 --> 01:48:42.180
engines, or quote "intelligent"
machines that really

01:48:42.180 --> 01:48:47.160
know and use semantics as we
study it are probably still far

01:48:47.160 --> 01:48:49.800
in the future.

01:48:49.800 --> 01:48:53.070
And in conclusion, I
would say I would suppose

01:48:53.070 --> 01:48:56.850
that really knowing
semantics is a prerequisite

01:48:56.850 --> 01:49:00.720
for any use of language
to be called intelligent.

01:49:00.720 --> 01:49:03.060
So if there is to be
a new basic research

01:49:03.060 --> 01:49:06.060
effort to understand
intelligence and the brain,

01:49:06.060 --> 01:49:09.450
semantics is ready to contribute
to it and to gain from it.

01:49:09.450 --> 01:49:10.406
Thank you.

01:49:19.397 --> 01:49:20.480
PINKER: Thank you Barbara.

01:49:20.480 --> 01:49:22.620
Patrick Winston, I
think you're going to be

01:49:22.620 --> 01:49:25.160
a motile organism, as I recall.

01:49:29.480 --> 01:49:31.410
Would you like to
use the podium?

01:49:31.410 --> 01:49:34.490
WINSTON: I think
I'll not use that.

01:49:37.220 --> 01:49:43.760
I've never been very good
at introductory statements,

01:49:43.760 --> 01:49:45.910
so I haven't prepared one.

01:49:45.910 --> 01:49:49.940
I'll ask myself a few
questions instead.

01:49:49.940 --> 01:49:54.110
My first question is, well,
what do you think so far?

01:49:54.110 --> 01:49:55.990
And that's a difficult
question for me

01:49:55.990 --> 01:49:58.990
to answer, because
several of the speakers,

01:49:58.990 --> 01:50:02.400
you may have noticed,
ran out of time.

01:50:02.400 --> 01:50:05.280
I was particularly
disappointed that Marvin

01:50:05.280 --> 01:50:11.010
was unable to give us a detailed
account of his assessment

01:50:11.010 --> 01:50:13.560
of the progress that's been
made in artificial intelligence

01:50:13.560 --> 01:50:16.650
in the past 20
years, so I decided

01:50:16.650 --> 01:50:19.230
to dedicate a little of my
time to doing a simulation

01:50:19.230 --> 01:50:25.420
of Marvin, and here it is.

01:50:28.640 --> 01:50:33.580
And that concludes my
simulation of Marvin's account

01:50:33.580 --> 01:50:37.290
of the past 20 years.

01:50:37.290 --> 01:50:41.032
Now it's certainly the
case that many people

01:50:41.032 --> 01:50:43.240
would contest the view that
there's been no progress,

01:50:43.240 --> 01:50:46.020
but I don't think anyone would
contest the view that there

01:50:46.020 --> 01:50:49.920
could have been more
progress in the past 20 years

01:50:49.920 --> 01:50:51.170
than there has been.

01:50:51.170 --> 01:50:55.650
And I think it's informative to
think about why that might be,

01:50:55.650 --> 01:50:58.110
because we have no lamp to
guide us into the future

01:50:58.110 --> 01:51:04.920
except by the lamp of what
has [? passed ?] before.

01:51:04.920 --> 01:51:09.540
And I think that in my
view, what went wrong

01:51:09.540 --> 01:51:12.202
went wrong in the '80s.

01:51:12.202 --> 01:51:13.660
And the first thing
that went wrong

01:51:13.660 --> 01:51:16.870
is that we all discovered
that artificial intelligence

01:51:16.870 --> 01:51:21.120
technology was actually
useful, and that

01:51:21.120 --> 01:51:25.770
led to a kind of
commercial distraction,

01:51:25.770 --> 01:51:28.573
from which the field
has never recovered.

01:51:28.573 --> 01:51:29.490
So that's one problem.

01:51:33.290 --> 01:51:35.990
Another problem, of
course, is that happened

01:51:35.990 --> 01:51:38.282
to be the decade in
which the Cold War ended.

01:51:38.282 --> 01:51:39.740
And as a consequence
of that, there

01:51:39.740 --> 01:51:41.510
were shifts in
funding patterns that

01:51:41.510 --> 01:51:45.410
contributed to the problem,
because sponsors were no longer

01:51:45.410 --> 01:51:49.227
as interested in being
princes of science,

01:51:49.227 --> 01:51:50.810
but rather became
much more interested

01:51:50.810 --> 01:51:55.310
in short-term goals,
demonstrations, and the like.

01:51:55.310 --> 01:51:57.040
But I think the most
insidious problem

01:51:57.040 --> 01:52:02.410
was what I'd like to label
the mechanistic Balkanization

01:52:02.410 --> 01:52:04.340
of the field.

01:52:04.340 --> 01:52:07.630
What happened in that period was
that artificial intelligence,

01:52:07.630 --> 01:52:12.130
as a field, matured
and began to break up

01:52:12.130 --> 01:52:17.380
into communities that were
coagulating around mechanisms

01:52:17.380 --> 01:52:19.690
rather than problems.

01:52:19.690 --> 01:52:21.490
So it was in the
'80s that we began

01:52:21.490 --> 01:52:27.200
to see conferences dedicated
to neural nets, conferences

01:52:27.200 --> 01:52:32.330
dedicated to genetic algorithms,
conferences dedicated

01:52:32.330 --> 01:52:35.210
to probabilistic inference.

01:52:35.210 --> 01:52:37.430
And when you dedicate
your conferences

01:52:37.430 --> 01:52:39.470
to subjects of that
kind of mechanisms,

01:52:39.470 --> 01:52:42.020
then there's a
tendency to not work

01:52:42.020 --> 01:52:44.630
on fundamental problems
but rather those problems

01:52:44.630 --> 01:52:48.660
that the mechanisms
can actually deal with.

01:52:48.660 --> 01:52:51.270
This tended to affect not
only the conferences that

01:52:51.270 --> 01:52:53.880
were being held, but also
the kind of jobs that

01:52:53.880 --> 01:52:57.450
were offered by universities.

01:52:57.450 --> 01:53:01.200
Those job descriptions tended
to have phrases in them

01:53:01.200 --> 01:53:06.960
like neural nets, probabilistic
inference, genetic algorithms,

01:53:06.960 --> 01:53:08.760
and the like.

01:53:08.760 --> 01:53:16.320
I think it was in about 1995
that I had a horrible nightmare

01:53:16.320 --> 01:53:17.370
as a consequence of this.

01:53:17.370 --> 01:53:20.225
I was on the search committee
for the Electrical Engineering

01:53:20.225 --> 01:53:23.170
and Computer Science
Department at the time.

01:53:23.170 --> 01:53:26.480
And in my nightmare,
we had put out

01:53:26.480 --> 01:53:29.120
a call for people interested
in making an application

01:53:29.120 --> 01:53:34.090
to work in MIT as a professor
in the Electrical Engineering

01:53:34.090 --> 01:53:36.770
and Computer Science Department.

01:53:36.770 --> 01:53:37.820
And Jesus applied.

01:53:40.570 --> 01:53:44.090
And after due consideration
of the application,

01:53:44.090 --> 01:53:48.070
we decided to send
the standard letter.

01:53:48.070 --> 01:53:51.420
Thank you for your
interest in our department.

01:53:51.420 --> 01:53:53.580
We regret that with
over 400 applicants,

01:53:53.580 --> 01:53:55.650
we cannot interview anyone.

01:53:55.650 --> 01:54:00.330
And besides, this year we're
not looking for a theologian.

01:54:00.330 --> 01:54:02.970
It was because of that
kind of Balkanization,

01:54:02.970 --> 01:54:08.430
which focused everyone
on particular rather

01:54:08.430 --> 01:54:12.120
narrow elements of the field.

01:54:12.120 --> 01:54:14.380
So what to do?

01:54:14.380 --> 01:54:16.780
Some people say well,
Winston, the trouble with you

01:54:16.780 --> 01:54:19.993
is you're just a hopeless
romantic about the past.

01:54:19.993 --> 01:54:21.910
But that's not entirely
true, because I'm also

01:54:21.910 --> 01:54:24.110
romantic about the future.

01:54:24.110 --> 01:54:26.100
It's just right now
that may not be so hot.

01:54:29.410 --> 01:54:35.620
And I think the future
does offer some hope.

01:54:35.620 --> 01:54:36.640
And you might say why?

01:54:36.640 --> 01:54:39.790
And I think the reason is
because we now are gradually

01:54:39.790 --> 01:54:42.680
becoming less stupid
about what to do.

01:54:42.680 --> 01:54:47.060
So you might say, well, what is
it that you think we should do?

01:54:47.060 --> 01:54:49.510
And then my response
would be well,

01:54:49.510 --> 01:54:51.820
I think we should take
the right approach.

01:54:51.820 --> 01:54:54.428
And then you would say
what is the right approach?

01:54:54.428 --> 01:54:55.720
What do you call your approach?

01:54:55.720 --> 01:54:59.183
And I would reply, well, I
call my approach the right way.

01:54:59.183 --> 01:55:00.600
And then you might
say, well, what

01:55:00.600 --> 01:55:03.222
does your approach
have to offer?

01:55:03.222 --> 01:55:05.680
And then I would say that my
approach, what my approach has

01:55:05.680 --> 01:55:10.000
to offer is a focus on the
fundamental differences

01:55:10.000 --> 01:55:15.700
between our human species
and other primates.

01:55:15.700 --> 01:55:20.175
And in particular to
others of the Homo genus.

01:55:20.175 --> 01:55:21.550
And you might say,
well, what are

01:55:21.550 --> 01:55:23.620
those properties that
separate us from,

01:55:23.620 --> 01:55:25.960
for instance, the Neanderthals?

01:55:25.960 --> 01:55:27.280
And who knows?

01:55:27.280 --> 01:55:29.710
But if we want a clue,
the best thing to do

01:55:29.710 --> 01:55:32.240
is to ask people who
study the question.

01:55:32.240 --> 01:55:36.980
So I've been much influenced
by, for example, the cultural--

01:55:36.980 --> 01:55:40.480
rather the paleoanthropologist
Ian Tattersall

01:55:40.480 --> 01:55:44.650
who tells us that
we, as a species,

01:55:44.650 --> 01:55:47.790
coexisted quite happily with
the Neanderthals for about

01:55:47.790 --> 01:55:49.660
100,000 years.

01:55:49.660 --> 01:55:52.480
And during that time, we made
approximately the same kind

01:55:52.480 --> 01:55:54.100
of stone tools.

01:55:54.100 --> 01:55:56.710
And during that time there
was approximately no progress

01:55:56.710 --> 01:55:59.690
in our making of stone tools.

01:55:59.690 --> 01:56:04.270
But then about 60,000 years
ago or so something magical

01:56:04.270 --> 01:56:06.400
happened to the human species.

01:56:06.400 --> 01:56:09.070
It probably happened
in southern Africa.

01:56:09.070 --> 01:56:11.740
And it probably happened
in a neck-down population

01:56:11.740 --> 01:56:14.230
of maybe many
hundred, but certainly

01:56:14.230 --> 01:56:16.660
not more than a few
thousand individuals.

01:56:16.660 --> 01:56:19.470
And once we got
that new property,

01:56:19.470 --> 01:56:22.110
it spread throughout
the human population.

01:56:22.110 --> 01:56:25.450
And within 10,000 years
we had either wiped out

01:56:25.450 --> 01:56:28.750
or outcompeted the
Neanderthals, wiped out

01:56:28.750 --> 01:56:31.570
a lot of species along our
path throughout the world

01:56:31.570 --> 01:56:33.370
and never looked back.

01:56:33.370 --> 01:56:37.180
And so what is it then that
was that magic property?

01:56:37.180 --> 01:56:38.290
Once again, who knows?

01:56:38.290 --> 01:56:40.490
But we can speculate.

01:56:40.490 --> 01:56:44.860
And I've heard Noam say things
like it was the capacity,

01:56:44.860 --> 01:56:48.730
perhaps, to take two concepts
and put them together to make

01:56:48.730 --> 01:56:51.790
a third concept, and to
do that without limit

01:56:51.790 --> 01:56:53.920
and without disturbing the
concepts that got merged

01:56:53.920 --> 01:56:56.050
together.

01:56:56.050 --> 01:56:57.710
I think that's probably right.

01:56:57.710 --> 01:57:01.480
And I would hazard to layer
a piece on top of that,

01:57:01.480 --> 01:57:03.670
because I think that
that capability, in turn,

01:57:03.670 --> 01:57:07.220
makes it possible for
us to describe events.

01:57:07.220 --> 01:57:09.110
And once we can
describe events, it's

01:57:09.110 --> 01:57:11.600
natural to develop a capacity
to stringing them together

01:57:11.600 --> 01:57:13.270
into stories.

01:57:13.270 --> 01:57:16.720
And once we have stories,
then we can take two stories

01:57:16.720 --> 01:57:19.400
and blend them together
to make another story.

01:57:19.400 --> 01:57:22.990
And once we have that, we have
at least a kind of creativity.

01:57:22.990 --> 01:57:27.640
And once we have that,
we have a species that's

01:57:27.640 --> 01:57:30.238
much different from any other.

01:57:30.238 --> 01:57:34.617
So I've come to believe that
that story understanding is

01:57:34.617 --> 01:57:36.200
one of the great
things that's enabled

01:57:36.200 --> 01:57:39.260
by the inner language that's
underneath our communication

01:57:39.260 --> 01:57:41.230
language.

01:57:41.230 --> 01:57:43.260
And I think that the
past 50,000 years

01:57:43.260 --> 01:57:45.870
has been a period of time
in which we have become

01:57:45.870 --> 01:57:48.720
increasingly awash in stories.

01:57:48.720 --> 01:57:51.840
We are soaked in stories
from our birth to our death.

01:57:51.840 --> 01:57:53.760
We start with fairy tales.

01:57:53.760 --> 01:57:56.190
We continue on through
religious parables.

01:57:56.190 --> 01:57:59.010
We go on to literature
and history.

01:57:59.010 --> 01:58:01.590
We end up perhaps with law
school or medical school.

01:58:01.590 --> 01:58:04.420
And throughout that whole
period of education,

01:58:04.420 --> 01:58:07.580
we are essentially
accumulating and learning

01:58:07.580 --> 01:58:11.510
to manipulate the stories
that come to us in our culture

01:58:11.510 --> 01:58:14.070
and in our environment.

01:58:14.070 --> 01:58:16.170
So that's what I think
ought to be done.

01:58:16.170 --> 01:58:21.020
And now you might ask an actual
question, what kind of property

01:58:21.020 --> 01:58:26.510
do you need in order to study
these particular questions that

01:58:26.510 --> 01:58:27.960
derive from this approach?

01:58:27.960 --> 01:58:29.960
And the answer is that
there's only one property

01:58:29.960 --> 01:58:34.120
that you really
need, and that is,

01:58:34.120 --> 01:58:37.960
I think, naive
optimism in the face

01:58:37.960 --> 01:58:42.220
of overwhelming
contraindication.

01:58:42.220 --> 01:58:43.090
But that's okay.

01:58:43.090 --> 01:58:45.200
I don't mind that,
because at the very least,

01:58:45.200 --> 01:58:47.440
I think that through
the study of stories

01:58:47.440 --> 01:58:50.830
that we will develop at least
sharper questions to ask

01:58:50.830 --> 01:58:53.270
in the next generation.

01:58:53.270 --> 01:58:57.210
I also think that it's possible
to do the kinds of things

01:58:57.210 --> 01:59:00.810
that I do better today
than I was able to do them

01:59:00.810 --> 01:59:04.200
some years ago, because I think
I've gradually become less

01:59:04.200 --> 01:59:08.160
stupid about the steps that need
to be taken in order to make

01:59:08.160 --> 01:59:09.443
progress.

01:59:09.443 --> 01:59:10.860
So here are the
steps that I think

01:59:10.860 --> 01:59:14.580
you need to take in
order to make progress.

01:59:14.580 --> 01:59:20.470
First of all, you need to
characterize the behavior.

01:59:20.470 --> 01:59:26.310
Next, after you've
characterized the behavior,

01:59:26.310 --> 01:59:29.440
you need to formulate a
computational problem.

01:59:29.440 --> 01:59:32.388
And once you've formulated
a computational problem,

01:59:32.388 --> 01:59:33.930
then you could take
step three, which

01:59:33.930 --> 01:59:35.860
is to propose a
computational solution.

01:59:35.860 --> 01:59:39.190
And all of these things
are the natural objectives

01:59:39.190 --> 01:59:41.360
of classical psychology.

01:59:41.360 --> 01:59:43.040
But then there's a fourth step.

01:59:43.040 --> 01:59:44.950
Once you have the
proposed solution

01:59:44.950 --> 01:59:47.740
to the computational problem,
then the kind of thing

01:59:47.740 --> 01:59:54.320
that I do I could refer to
as exploratory development,

01:59:54.320 --> 01:59:56.620
building systems
that you believe

01:59:56.620 --> 02:00:00.280
will have the behavior
that you have characterized

02:00:00.280 --> 02:00:03.640
in virtue of the
computational solution

02:00:03.640 --> 02:00:06.830
that you have proposed.

02:00:06.830 --> 02:00:09.410
And when you do that, you
discover, to your horror

02:00:09.410 --> 02:00:11.560
and amazement, that
you left things out,

02:00:11.560 --> 02:00:13.400
that your solution
is incomplete,

02:00:13.400 --> 02:00:16.010
and you have to go back
through that cycle again,

02:00:16.010 --> 02:00:18.140
and again, and again.

02:00:18.140 --> 02:00:19.670
So I think that's four steps.

02:00:19.670 --> 02:00:24.220
The computational problem--
rather the behavior,

02:00:24.220 --> 02:00:27.410
the computational problem with
all of the constraint necessary

02:00:27.410 --> 02:00:29.840
to solve it, a
proposed solution,

02:00:29.840 --> 02:00:32.660
exploratory development,
and as one of my students,

02:00:32.660 --> 02:00:36.620
[? Leonard ?] [? Federov, ?]
mentioned to me this morning,

02:00:36.620 --> 02:00:41.198
that isn't enough either,
because at step four,

02:00:41.198 --> 02:00:43.490
you might be in the same
situation that the Romans were

02:00:43.490 --> 02:00:45.260
when they had catapults.

02:00:45.260 --> 02:00:47.960
You can improve them, but
you didn't understand them

02:00:47.960 --> 02:00:52.410
until you had a notion
of Newtonian mechanics.

02:00:52.410 --> 02:00:54.390
So the fifth step,
then, is you have

02:00:54.390 --> 02:00:56.580
to look back over
the first four steps

02:00:56.580 --> 02:00:58.620
and crystallize out
the principles that

02:00:58.620 --> 02:01:01.380
made everything possible.

02:01:01.380 --> 02:01:05.410
So that's what I think
ought to be done.

02:01:05.410 --> 02:01:08.180
And that's the sort
of thing that I do.

02:01:08.180 --> 02:01:09.920
But as I say that,
I think it would

02:01:09.920 --> 02:01:12.800
be easy to suppose
that I think everything

02:01:12.800 --> 02:01:17.090
happens in symbol
land, merely because we

02:01:17.090 --> 02:01:19.003
are a symbolic species.

02:01:19.003 --> 02:01:21.170
But I don't think everything
happens in symbol land,

02:01:21.170 --> 02:01:24.230
because I think we make
use of the stuff that

02:01:24.230 --> 02:01:26.260
was there before.

02:01:26.260 --> 02:01:29.080
In particular, we have to
solve a lot of problems

02:01:29.080 --> 02:01:32.340
with our perceptual apparatus.

02:01:32.340 --> 02:01:38.020
So I could say, for example,
to you, Sebastian kissed Erin.

02:01:38.020 --> 02:01:41.760
And then I could ask did
Sebastian touch Erin?

02:01:41.760 --> 02:01:44.320
And you would immediately
reply yes, of course,

02:01:44.320 --> 02:01:45.890
because you can imagine it.

02:01:45.890 --> 02:01:49.110
And you don't feel like you're
doing syllogistic reasoning.

02:01:49.110 --> 02:01:51.870
You feel like you're
imagining the situation

02:01:51.870 --> 02:01:58.260
and reading the answer off of
the imagined situation with you

02:01:58.260 --> 02:02:00.480
with your perceptual apparatus.

02:02:00.480 --> 02:02:02.370
And I think that's right.

02:02:02.370 --> 02:02:05.060
I think we know a lot
of things latently

02:02:05.060 --> 02:02:07.120
and we don't have
to be told them.

02:02:07.120 --> 02:02:08.620
We don't have to
be told that if you

02:02:08.620 --> 02:02:11.920
kiss somebody you touch them,
because we can figure that out

02:02:11.920 --> 02:02:13.375
on the fly.

02:02:13.375 --> 02:02:14.875
And then, of course,
we can cache it

02:02:14.875 --> 02:02:16.840
in our symbolic
systems if we want,

02:02:16.840 --> 02:02:19.150
but the point is that we
can compute that stuff when

02:02:19.150 --> 02:02:20.090
we need it.

02:02:20.090 --> 02:02:24.340
So we know vastly more
than we actually know.

02:02:24.340 --> 02:02:27.130
Here's another example
that I am very fond of.

02:02:27.130 --> 02:02:31.030
I borrowed it from [? Shimon ?]
[? Oman. ?] It's the example

02:02:31.030 --> 02:02:35.530
of, well, what am I doing?

02:02:38.630 --> 02:02:40.508
It's not a trick question.

02:02:40.508 --> 02:02:41.050
I'm drinking.

02:02:43.640 --> 02:02:45.880
Now what am I doing?

02:02:45.880 --> 02:02:48.290
I'm toasting.

02:02:48.290 --> 02:02:51.290
Now what am I doing?

02:02:51.290 --> 02:02:56.050
I'm admiring this fine
[INAUDIBLE] glass.

02:02:56.050 --> 02:02:58.780
But then for the
next example I have

02:02:58.780 --> 02:03:01.600
to ask you to exercise your
own power of imagination,

02:03:01.600 --> 02:03:04.550
because I don't have
a slide that does it.

02:03:04.550 --> 02:03:09.440
What I want you to
imagine is that I'm a cat,

02:03:09.440 --> 02:03:13.370
and my mouth is
pointed to the sky.

02:03:13.370 --> 02:03:16.880
And up there above my
mouth is a leaking faucet.

02:03:20.500 --> 02:03:22.520
What am I doing?

02:03:22.520 --> 02:03:24.300
I'm drinking.

02:03:24.300 --> 02:03:27.030
Even though that kind of
drinking doesn't look,

02:03:27.030 --> 02:03:30.330
visually, anything like
the kind of drinking I

02:03:30.330 --> 02:03:33.300
did with my first
example, yet somehow we

02:03:33.300 --> 02:03:36.300
label those both as drinking.

02:03:36.300 --> 02:03:39.480
So my view is that
it must be the case

02:03:39.480 --> 02:03:42.840
that this kind of drinking
and that kind of drinking

02:03:42.840 --> 02:03:44.950
is telling the same story.

02:03:44.950 --> 02:03:47.130
That there's a
story about thirst.

02:03:47.130 --> 02:03:49.510
That there's a
story about motion.

02:03:49.510 --> 02:03:54.420
There's a story about
liquid moving through space

02:03:54.420 --> 02:03:55.560
into your mouth.

02:03:55.560 --> 02:03:57.000
And that's the
story of drinking,

02:03:57.000 --> 02:03:59.880
and you can recognize it from
a single frame as a drinking

02:03:59.880 --> 02:04:03.850
story even though it's very
different from other frames,

02:04:03.850 --> 02:04:09.840
from other videos you've seen
that tell the drinking story.

02:04:09.840 --> 02:04:11.528
So that's what I think.

02:04:11.528 --> 02:04:13.070
And the last question
I'll ask myself

02:04:13.070 --> 02:04:15.360
is what I've tried to
contribute here today.

02:04:15.360 --> 02:04:19.070
And the answer is I tried to
talk a little bit about why

02:04:19.070 --> 02:04:21.830
I think we haven't made progress
in the field to the extent

02:04:21.830 --> 02:04:24.920
that we should have, what
I think we ought to do now

02:04:24.920 --> 02:04:28.320
in order to make progress,
and the steps that I think

02:04:28.320 --> 02:04:30.750
need to be taken
by people who are

02:04:30.750 --> 02:04:32.610
trying to make that progress.

02:04:32.610 --> 02:04:36.450
And an emphasis, too, on
the idea that we're not just

02:04:36.450 --> 02:04:40.080
a symbolic species
[? or ?] symbolic nature,

02:04:40.080 --> 02:04:42.935
and that our [? interlanguage ?]
capability make possible

02:04:42.935 --> 02:04:45.060
an interaction with our
perceptual apparatus that I

02:04:45.060 --> 02:04:49.320
think would not be possible
in a dog or a chimpanzee.

02:04:49.320 --> 02:04:57.090
And finally, I think as part
of the contribution package,

02:04:57.090 --> 02:04:59.970
I'd like to say that I think
that if we do all this, then

02:04:59.970 --> 02:05:06.270
we'll have a much better
understanding of how we work.

02:05:06.270 --> 02:05:08.670
And I think that
will lead to what

02:05:08.670 --> 02:05:12.210
I would like to call, with
perhaps a touch of irony,

02:05:12.210 --> 02:05:14.370
unthinkable value
to the species.

02:05:27.260 --> 02:05:29.330
PINKER: Thank you, Patrick.

02:05:29.330 --> 02:05:32.450
Tommy Poggio tells me that
we have another 15 minutes,

02:05:32.450 --> 02:05:34.280
and I would hate to
let this opportunity

02:05:34.280 --> 02:05:37.850
pass with this extraordinary
collection of speakers

02:05:37.850 --> 02:05:40.910
without some exploration
of perhaps some themes that

02:05:40.910 --> 02:05:44.120
cross-cut some of
the presentations.

02:05:44.120 --> 02:05:48.230
I'm going to take the liberty
of asking a couple of questions

02:05:48.230 --> 02:05:51.050
myself to try to draw
out some connections

02:05:51.050 --> 02:05:54.200
among the fascinating
presentations we just heard.

02:05:54.200 --> 02:05:57.350
And I'll start with
Sydney Brenner.

02:05:57.350 --> 02:06:03.200
You have inaugurated the
study of the nervous system

02:06:03.200 --> 02:06:06.920
of a creature with 302 neurons.

02:06:06.920 --> 02:06:11.180
Many of us are interested in the
neuroscience of a creature with

02:06:11.180 --> 02:06:13.880
[? 100 ?] billion
[? 302 ?] neurons,

02:06:13.880 --> 02:06:16.150
give or take a few 100,000.

02:06:16.150 --> 02:06:21.080
And we have heard,
this very evening,

02:06:21.080 --> 02:06:25.700
suggestions that the genetic
code contains information

02:06:25.700 --> 02:06:28.850
that is exploited in the
development of the capacity,

02:06:28.850 --> 02:06:30.980
say, to use human
language together

02:06:30.980 --> 02:06:36.020
with the thoughts that
are expressed in language.

02:06:36.020 --> 02:06:42.160
You referred jocularly
to the Chomsky gene,

02:06:42.160 --> 02:06:45.770
the single hypothetical gene
that turns us into a human,

02:06:45.770 --> 02:06:49.700
but more seriously, do
you have any thoughts

02:06:49.700 --> 02:06:53.360
on how we might eventually
narrow the gap between systems

02:06:53.360 --> 02:06:55.850
like C. elegans,
where we clearly

02:06:55.850 --> 02:06:57.620
have an enormous amount
of understanding,

02:06:57.620 --> 02:07:00.470
and the organism that
many of the rest of us

02:07:00.470 --> 02:07:03.710
are interested in and
the possible information

02:07:03.710 --> 02:07:06.530
in the genome that results
in the innate unfolding

02:07:06.530 --> 02:07:08.180
of cognitive abilities?

02:07:11.660 --> 02:07:13.220
BRENNER: Well, I
think it has to go

02:07:13.220 --> 02:07:18.500
beyond these hardwired systems.

02:07:18.500 --> 02:07:25.010
And I think there is a
biological invention that

02:07:25.010 --> 02:07:33.290
occurred, which allowed us
to expand the possibilities

02:07:33.290 --> 02:07:35.700
of the system.

02:07:35.700 --> 02:07:40.950
If I can give one
example of this,

02:07:40.950 --> 02:07:48.660
I think that it would be
extremely interesting to ask

02:07:48.660 --> 02:07:54.690
is it just going to be more
of the same stuff that somehow

02:07:54.690 --> 02:07:59.370
culminates in intelligence
and all the other properties

02:07:59.370 --> 02:08:01.100
associated?

02:08:01.100 --> 02:08:06.350
Or is there some revolutionary
change in [? principal? ?]

02:08:06.350 --> 02:08:08.600
So I think the
important thing is

02:08:08.600 --> 02:08:14.320
in that once we get away and
have a way of building bigger

02:08:14.320 --> 02:08:20.500
nervous systems, we have
the potential, then,

02:08:20.500 --> 02:08:24.250
to have not fix it.

02:08:24.250 --> 02:08:27.600
So there are many such
systems in biology.

02:08:27.600 --> 02:08:29.670
The immune system is.

02:08:29.670 --> 02:08:34.170
It says you can learn
things from the past, which

02:08:34.170 --> 02:08:38.310
is [? true ?] by animals being
selected because of things,

02:08:38.310 --> 02:08:42.720
but you must have
a way of meeting

02:08:42.720 --> 02:08:45.760
unforeseen circumstances.

02:08:45.760 --> 02:08:48.165
And I think we have to
have something like that.

02:08:48.165 --> 02:08:51.550
It needn't be coded
like the immune system.

02:08:51.550 --> 02:08:56.690
We have to have a flexible
system in which we can achieve

02:08:56.690 --> 02:09:02.160
the result but we need
additional input of experience,

02:09:02.160 --> 02:09:03.540
if you like.

02:09:03.540 --> 02:09:11.190
So perhaps our genomes only
decide how to make a baby

02:09:11.190 --> 02:09:13.890
and all the rest of
it has to follow.

02:09:13.890 --> 02:09:17.800
But [? they ?] give you the
capacity to do these things.

02:09:17.800 --> 02:09:19.890
And that is the--

02:09:19.890 --> 02:09:23.230
that, I think, is the
very interesting thing.

02:09:23.230 --> 02:09:25.890
On the other hand,
I am still very

02:09:25.890 --> 02:09:30.720
interested in the
accumulation that

02:09:30.720 --> 02:09:33.960
comes from the
processes of evolution.

02:09:33.960 --> 02:09:39.300
And evolution, of course,
naturally selects for organisms

02:09:39.300 --> 02:09:44.280
and, so to speak,
guarantees their survival.

02:09:44.280 --> 02:09:49.560
In fact, it could be argued
that the trouble with us,

02:09:49.560 --> 02:09:54.560
as human beings, is that we
stopped biological evolution,

02:09:54.560 --> 02:09:57.270
[? where ?] sitting
inside of ourselves

02:09:57.270 --> 02:10:02.370
is a quarter of a million year
old genome, totally unsuited,

02:10:02.370 --> 02:10:07.450
maladapted to the environment
we need to create--

02:10:07.450 --> 02:10:12.370
that we've created
as part of history.

02:10:12.370 --> 02:10:17.310
And I think we should be clear
about that, because there

02:10:17.310 --> 02:10:23.010
is still, if you like, deep
down inside there is an animal.

02:10:23.010 --> 02:10:27.120
And the animal is still very
much like a rat or a mouse.

02:10:27.120 --> 02:10:29.890
Of course it doesn't
do the same things,

02:10:29.890 --> 02:10:35.730
but it is designed, a system
designed for function,

02:10:35.730 --> 02:10:38.070
for survival.

02:10:38.070 --> 02:10:40.440
I think what we've
been able to do

02:10:40.440 --> 02:10:45.390
by the expansion of other
parts of the brain is,

02:10:45.390 --> 02:10:50.790
in fact, in many cases,
to be able to suppress it.

02:10:50.790 --> 02:10:53.430
I think there's a
very good example.

02:10:53.430 --> 02:10:56.650
You can see this very clearly.

02:10:56.650 --> 02:11:00.510
And that's why I think the
basic part of the brain

02:11:00.510 --> 02:11:03.570
that we can still
explain from the genome

02:11:03.570 --> 02:11:08.140
and all these other things
resides in the hypothalamus.

02:11:08.140 --> 02:11:13.140
The hypothalamus is
Freud's id, all right?

02:11:13.140 --> 02:11:16.350
Now what happens is
the frontal cortex

02:11:16.350 --> 02:11:21.180
sends inhibitory stimulus
down to the hypothalamus

02:11:21.180 --> 02:11:25.110
and says stop
behaving like a beast.

02:11:25.110 --> 02:11:29.940
So all those things which we
regret in our constitution,

02:11:29.940 --> 02:11:35.580
like greed, lust,
desire, avarice,

02:11:35.580 --> 02:11:39.660
those are encoded,
really, in the genes

02:11:39.660 --> 02:11:42.280
that affect the hypothalamus.

02:11:42.280 --> 02:11:45.100
What we have is this
inhibitory thing.

02:11:45.100 --> 02:11:49.080
So that means that
we can suppress this

02:11:49.080 --> 02:11:53.950
and sometimes we don't
want to or can't and so on.

02:11:53.950 --> 02:11:57.260
So I think that the layering
of the system, which it

02:11:57.260 --> 02:11:58.530
distinguishes that.

02:11:58.530 --> 02:12:03.600
And therefore, you will have
to work in this field here

02:12:03.600 --> 02:12:06.120
that looks at this.

02:12:06.120 --> 02:12:09.030
Everybody knows
that you can take--

02:12:09.030 --> 02:12:14.430
if you take a rat and let it
smell a cat, has to smell it,

02:12:14.430 --> 02:12:20.645
that's encoded in the genome and
it undergoes a complete panic.

02:12:20.645 --> 02:12:25.440
Of course you can condition
that to the vision of a cat.

02:12:25.440 --> 02:12:29.430
Then you show the animal
a cat, complete panic.

02:12:29.430 --> 02:12:32.030
You take it out and
you just destroy

02:12:32.030 --> 02:12:37.800
3,000 cells in the hypothalamus
and you put it back

02:12:37.800 --> 02:12:38.700
in the cage.

02:12:38.700 --> 02:12:41.002
You can show it the cat.

02:12:41.002 --> 02:12:45.370
It'll saunter out of this,
walk in front of the cat.

02:12:45.370 --> 02:12:50.590
The amusing thing, now
the cat is perplexed.

02:12:50.590 --> 02:12:52.740
However, I'll just
give you that.

02:12:52.740 --> 02:12:57.130
And I think a lot
of this is in this,

02:12:57.130 --> 02:13:01.000
and I think this is where
biology can tell you

02:13:01.000 --> 02:13:05.620
what the ground process is,
what's encoded in our genes.

02:13:05.620 --> 02:13:08.150
Many things are like that.

02:13:08.150 --> 02:13:11.860
But I think the rest of
it is all the other stuff

02:13:11.860 --> 02:13:13.220
that we've seen.

02:13:13.220 --> 02:13:17.860
So we have, if you like, a
machine within a machine.

02:13:17.860 --> 02:13:22.360
We have [? then ?] added on
top of this a machine which is

02:13:22.360 --> 02:13:27.610
capable of flexibility,
[? and ?] is capable

02:13:27.610 --> 02:13:29.170
of changing it.

02:13:29.170 --> 02:13:34.150
So that's why I don't
believe that tracing out

02:13:34.150 --> 02:13:36.220
all the connections--

02:13:36.220 --> 02:13:38.800
I think that's a
form of insanity.

02:13:38.800 --> 02:13:42.920
And then we'll be able
to discover exactly this.

02:13:42.920 --> 02:13:46.840
I think that is a
form of insanity.

02:13:46.840 --> 02:13:52.045
I mean, today, nobody can
describe to you in detail

02:13:52.045 --> 02:13:57.640
all the physical events that
occur in a computer chip

02:13:57.640 --> 02:14:01.780
when you take a
picture in your camera

02:14:01.780 --> 02:14:05.590
which has the telephone
attachment to it.

02:14:05.590 --> 02:14:09.730
And nobody wants to
describe it anymore,

02:14:09.730 --> 02:14:12.440
because the basic
principles are there.

02:14:12.440 --> 02:14:14.020
We've got a [? buffet. ?]

02:14:14.020 --> 02:14:17.110
So I think we have to
look at this [? still ?]

02:14:17.110 --> 02:14:20.860
with the whole thing of
layers of organization.

02:14:20.860 --> 02:14:24.370
And we must learn to
know what we can do

02:14:24.370 --> 02:14:28.040
and what others can
do together with us.

02:14:28.040 --> 02:14:32.500
That's why it's got to
be multiple discipline.

02:14:32.500 --> 02:14:33.790
PINKER: Thank you.

02:14:33.790 --> 02:14:36.905
I have a question for
both Noam and Marvin

02:14:36.905 --> 02:14:39.280
that I think is on the minds
of many people in this room,

02:14:39.280 --> 02:14:41.572
and I know it's been expressed
in some of the questions

02:14:41.572 --> 02:14:45.430
that I've received by
email prior to this event.

02:14:45.430 --> 02:14:49.810
There is a narrative in
which the new direction

02:14:49.810 --> 02:14:53.440
of both artificial intelligence
and cognitive science

02:14:53.440 --> 02:14:56.290
is one that makes
a great deal more

02:14:56.290 --> 02:14:59.620
use of probabilistic
information that

02:14:59.620 --> 02:15:04.000
is gleaned from enormous amounts
of experience during learning.

02:15:04.000 --> 02:15:07.930
This is manifested in
branches of cognitive science

02:15:07.930 --> 02:15:11.965
such as neural networks
and connectionism,

02:15:11.965 --> 02:15:15.130
Bayesian inference models,
application of machine

02:15:15.130 --> 02:15:16.730
learning to intelligence.

02:15:16.730 --> 02:15:20.230
Many of the models, both
of Tommy Poggio and Josh

02:15:20.230 --> 02:15:25.270
Tenenbaum, in the classic
work from the golden age,

02:15:25.270 --> 02:15:27.530
and indeed in many of
the models since then,

02:15:27.530 --> 02:15:29.980
including the models
of generative grammar

02:15:29.980 --> 02:15:33.460
and models of semantic
memory, probabilities

02:15:33.460 --> 02:15:35.410
don't play a big role.

02:15:35.410 --> 02:15:38.680
Is the narrative that says
that the direction of the field

02:15:38.680 --> 02:15:41.410
is in making use
of massive amounts

02:15:41.410 --> 02:15:44.950
of statistical
information via learning--

02:15:44.950 --> 02:15:47.840
well, maybe I'll ask you
to complete that sentence.

02:15:47.840 --> 02:15:50.990
What is the-- well, I'll let
you complete the sentence.

02:15:50.990 --> 02:15:51.490
Noam?

02:15:58.410 --> 02:15:59.240
CHOMSKY: It's true.

02:15:59.240 --> 02:16:01.690
There's been a lot
of work on trying

02:16:01.690 --> 02:16:09.980
to apply statistical models to
various linguistic problems.

02:16:09.980 --> 02:16:11.830
I think there have
been some successes,

02:16:11.830 --> 02:16:13.670
but a lot of failures.

02:16:13.670 --> 02:16:16.030
These successes, to
my knowledge at least,

02:16:16.030 --> 02:16:17.690
there's no time to
go into details,

02:16:17.690 --> 02:16:20.140
but the successes
that I know of are

02:16:20.140 --> 02:16:24.400
those that integrate
statistical analysis

02:16:24.400 --> 02:16:29.800
with some universal
grammar property,

02:16:29.800 --> 02:16:31.600
some fundamental
properties of language.

02:16:31.600 --> 02:16:33.850
When they're integrated, you
sometimes do get results.

02:16:37.049 --> 02:16:44.049
The simplest case, maybe, is
the problem of identifying words

02:16:44.049 --> 02:16:47.290
in a running discourse,
something apparently

02:16:47.290 --> 02:16:48.420
children do, you know?

02:16:48.420 --> 02:16:51.520
They hear a running discourse,
they pick out units.

02:16:51.520 --> 02:16:53.680
And the obvious units
are words, which

02:16:53.680 --> 02:16:56.670
are really phonological units.

02:16:56.670 --> 02:17:02.690
And there's a natural
property that--

02:17:02.690 --> 02:17:05.209
I wrote about it in the 1950s.

02:17:05.209 --> 02:17:06.940
I mean, it was just
taken for granted

02:17:06.940 --> 02:17:09.610
that if you just
take a look at the--

02:17:09.610 --> 02:17:12.340
if you have a series
of sounds and you

02:17:12.340 --> 02:17:16.330
look at the transitional
probabilities at each point,

02:17:16.330 --> 02:17:21.400
what's likely to come next,
when you get to a word boundary,

02:17:21.400 --> 02:17:22.653
the probabilities go way down.

02:17:22.653 --> 02:17:24.070
You don't know
what's coming next.

02:17:24.070 --> 02:17:27.430
If it's internal to a word,
you can predict pretty well.

02:17:27.430 --> 02:17:29.860
So if you kind of trace
transitional probabilities,

02:17:29.860 --> 02:17:32.349
you ought to get something
like word boundaries.

02:17:32.349 --> 02:17:35.110
Actually, I wrote
about it in 1955.

02:17:35.110 --> 02:17:36.549
And I assumed that
that's correct.

02:17:36.549 --> 02:17:38.920
Turns out to be false.

02:17:38.920 --> 02:17:42.820
It was shown, actually, by
Charles Yang, a former student

02:17:42.820 --> 02:17:46.420
here, [? got the ?] PhD in the
Computer Science Department,

02:17:46.420 --> 02:17:52.959
that if you apply that method,
it basically gives syllables

02:17:52.959 --> 02:17:55.059
in a language like English.

02:17:55.059 --> 02:17:57.910
On the other hand, if
you apply that message

02:17:57.910 --> 02:18:01.180
under a constraint,
namely the constraint

02:18:01.180 --> 02:18:05.780
that a word has what's
called a prosodic peak,

02:18:05.780 --> 02:18:11.120
you know, like a pitch
stress peak, which is true,

02:18:11.120 --> 02:18:13.760
then you get much
better results.

02:18:13.760 --> 02:18:17.520
Now there's more recent work,
which is still in [? press, ?]

02:18:17.520 --> 02:18:19.660
by [? Shukla, ?]
[? Aslund, ?] and others,

02:18:19.660 --> 02:18:23.920
which shows that you get still
better results if you apply

02:18:23.920 --> 02:18:28.760
that together with what are
called prosodic phrases.

02:18:28.760 --> 02:18:32.469
And it turns out that
a sentence, let's say,

02:18:32.469 --> 02:18:40.660
has units of pitch, and stress,
and so on, which are connected,

02:18:40.660 --> 02:18:43.180
related to the
syntactic structure.

02:18:43.180 --> 02:18:47.980
Actually in ways which were
studied, maybe first seriously,

02:18:47.980 --> 02:18:53.020
by another former student, Lisa
Selkirk a colleague of Barbara.

02:18:53.020 --> 02:18:57.219
But when you connect, when
you interact prosodic peaks

02:18:57.219 --> 02:18:59.650
with transitional
probabilities, then you

02:18:59.650 --> 02:19:03.760
get a pretty good identification
of word boundaries.

02:19:03.760 --> 02:19:05.379
Well, you know that's
the kind of work

02:19:05.379 --> 02:19:06.760
that I think makes sense.

02:19:09.309 --> 02:19:11.719
And there are more
complex examples,

02:19:11.719 --> 02:19:17.123
but it's a simple example of
the kind of thing that can work.

02:19:17.123 --> 02:19:18.790
On the other hand,
there's a lot of work

02:19:18.790 --> 02:19:22.980
which tries to do sophisticated
statistical analysis,

02:19:22.980 --> 02:19:25.240
you know, Bayesian,
and so on and so forth,

02:19:25.240 --> 02:19:30.549
without any concern for the
actual structure of language.

02:19:30.549 --> 02:19:34.990
As far as I'm aware, that
only achieves success

02:19:34.990 --> 02:19:38.420
in a very odd sense of success.

02:19:38.420 --> 02:19:41.200
There is this notion
of success, which

02:19:41.200 --> 02:19:44.350
has developed in
computational cognitive

02:19:44.350 --> 02:19:46.240
science in recent
years, which I think

02:19:46.240 --> 02:19:49.310
is novel in the
history of science.

02:19:49.310 --> 02:19:56.210
It interprets success as
approximating unanalyzed data.

02:19:56.210 --> 02:19:57.850
So for example, if
you were to study

02:19:57.850 --> 02:20:00.430
bee communication
this way, instead

02:20:00.430 --> 02:20:04.330
of doing the complex experiments
that bee scientists do,

02:20:04.330 --> 02:20:07.750
you know, like having
bees fly to an island

02:20:07.750 --> 02:20:10.930
to see if they leave an odor
trail and this sort of thing.

02:20:10.930 --> 02:20:15.940
If you simply did extensive
videotaping of bees

02:20:15.940 --> 02:20:20.230
swarming, okay, and you did
a lot of statistical analysis

02:20:20.230 --> 02:20:23.140
of it, you would get a
pretty good prediction

02:20:23.140 --> 02:20:27.070
for what bees are likely
to do next time they swarm.

02:20:27.070 --> 02:20:30.030
Actually get a better prediction
than bee scientists do,

02:20:30.030 --> 02:20:33.540
and they wouldn't care, because
they're not trying to do that.

02:20:33.540 --> 02:20:36.520
And you can make it a better
and better approximation

02:20:36.520 --> 02:20:40.220
by more videotapes and
more statistics and so on.

02:20:40.220 --> 02:20:42.670
I mean, actually, you
could do physics this way.

02:20:42.670 --> 02:20:45.940
Instead of studying things
like balls rolling down

02:20:45.940 --> 02:20:49.250
frictionless planes, which
can't happen in nature,

02:20:49.250 --> 02:20:52.600
if you took a ton of
videotapes of what's

02:20:52.600 --> 02:20:54.490
happening outside
my office window,

02:20:54.490 --> 02:20:58.010
let's say, bees flying
and various things,

02:20:58.010 --> 02:21:01.010
and you did an extensive
analysis of them,

02:21:01.010 --> 02:21:03.020
you would get some
kind of prediction

02:21:03.020 --> 02:21:05.720
of what's likely to
happen next, certainly way

02:21:05.720 --> 02:21:08.720
better than anybody in the
physics department could give.

02:21:08.720 --> 02:21:12.260
Well, that's a notion of
success, which is, I think,

02:21:12.260 --> 02:21:13.520
novel.

02:21:13.520 --> 02:21:17.590
I don't know of anything like
it in the history of science.

02:21:17.590 --> 02:21:20.700
And in those terms, you
get some kind of successes.

02:21:20.700 --> 02:21:23.120
And if you look at the
literature in the field,

02:21:23.120 --> 02:21:26.260
a lot of these papers
are listed as successes.

02:21:26.260 --> 02:21:28.340
And when you look
at them carefully,

02:21:28.340 --> 02:21:31.410
they're successes in
this particular sense,

02:21:31.410 --> 02:21:34.710
not the sense that science
has ever been interested in.

02:21:34.710 --> 02:21:38.550
But it does give you
ways of approximating

02:21:38.550 --> 02:21:42.450
unanalyzed data, analysis
corpus, and so on and so forth.

02:21:42.450 --> 02:21:44.890
I don't know of any
other cases, frankly.

02:21:44.890 --> 02:21:48.200
So there are successes where
things are integrated with some

02:21:48.200 --> 02:21:51.200
of the properties of
language, but I know

02:21:51.200 --> 02:21:53.450
of none in which they're not.

02:21:53.450 --> 02:21:54.200
PINKER: Thank you.

02:21:54.200 --> 02:21:56.522
To my tremendous
regret, and I imagine

02:21:56.522 --> 02:21:57.980
of many people in
the audience, I'm

02:21:57.980 --> 02:22:00.350
going to have to bring the
proceedings to a close,

02:22:00.350 --> 02:22:05.120
and we'll have to be left
with Patrick's interpretation

02:22:05.120 --> 02:22:09.620
of what Marvin's response would
have been to the question.

02:22:09.620 --> 02:22:12.140
I'd like to thank Sydney
Brenner, Marvin Minsky, Noam

02:22:12.140 --> 02:22:15.620
Chomsky, Emilio Bizzi, Barbara
Partee, Patrick Winston

02:22:15.620 --> 02:22:17.960
for an enormously
stimulating session.

02:22:17.960 --> 02:22:20.830
And thanks to all of you.

